WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:12.280
- I wanna welcome everyone
to this session of Endeavors,

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.240
which is the department colloquium

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:17.500
for African-American Studies
here at Yale University.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:20.750
I'm on Jacqueline Goldsby,
I'm chair of the department

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:23.840
and on behalf of our faculty,
our graduate students,

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:25.390
our undergraduate students, and staff

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:26.750
I wanna welcome everyone here.

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:30.580
It's exciting to see faces and
names that I don't recognize

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:32.490
and that's exactly the kind of outreach

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:36.193
we want to accomplish with this series.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:39.990
Since I wanna say a couple of things

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:43.157
about the series itself, what it is,

00:00:43.157 --> 00:00:45.810
what's on deck for us this semester,

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.600
the remainder of the semester

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:51.840
and a couple of reminders
about Zoom etiquette,

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.970
how we're going to be running
this particular session.

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:59.290
I'll start that now by
saying, as you all are now,

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:01.430
probably your mics are muted

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:03.400
and we'd ask you to keep your mics muted

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:07.110
throughout the discussion today.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:09.360
It's okay, as some of you are doing

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.260
to keep your videos turned off.

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:17.040
Zoom fatigue is real and the
important things to be engaged

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.120
with your mind and your thinking

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.990
and which leads to the
third Zoom etiquette point

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:26.560
that we will be using
the chat very actively

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:27.810
during this session

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.000
that will be the conduit and
in our other hosts today.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.540
Professor Crystal
Feimster will explain this

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:37.200
in a little bit more detail,
but I just want to say now,

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.580
that we'll be using the chat function

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:41.710
to talk with one another.

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:45.660
You can post ideas, thoughts,
questions, reflections.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.200
If you want to pose a question

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.220
directly to our speaker today,

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:52.710
you would send that question
or query that comment

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:56.033
to Professor Feimster through the chat.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:58.550
But those are some of the ground rules

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:00.363
for our discussion today.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:04.270
But again, Endeavors is
the department colloquium

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:05.980
for African-American Studies.

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:08.730
And for those of you who are
with us for the first time

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:13.330
there are two hallmarks to our colloquium.

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:16.370
First of all, we all gathered together

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:18.820
because instead of
separating out our colloquium

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:20.940
according to subfields,

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.010
we understand our work in black studies

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:24.840
to be interdisciplinary.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:29.840
So we deliberately have one
forum where we bring together

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:34.166
leading critics, scholars,
artists, activists,

00:02:34.166 --> 00:02:36.470
to present work in progress,

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:38.700
work that they are doing right now,

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.070
so that when they come
together and think with us,

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:44.823
we can help them sharpen
their ideas, their practice,

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:48.100
the projects that they're engaged in

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:52.270
and we in turn can bring their
methods, their questions,

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:54.930
their energy to the work we're doing

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:57.070
in our own respective fields.

00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:01.860
So two hallmarks about this colloquium.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.940
It's about work happening
now, and it's in progress,

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:09.160
and it's about interdisciplinarity.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:13.380
This semester, the
theme of our colloquium,

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:16.970
typically we allow ourselves
to kind of range across

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:18.380
the field of black studies

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:21.060
and to invite who's ever
doing the most exciting work

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:22.673
that we want to think with.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:27.270
But given the exigencies
that we face right now,

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:29.363
the ways in which we are
rake reckoning, right?

00:03:29.363 --> 00:03:33.130
This is the term with all of the ways

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:37.030
in which race has made an
unmade the modern world

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:40.210
and the particular face
of black people within it.

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:43.644
We are deciding to focus our attention

00:03:43.644 --> 00:03:48.590
on what it means to be black
now and to think about,

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:52.530
and to hold conversations towards
the change, the revolution

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:57.160
that needs to happen in so
many different areas of life

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:00.790
of one of which will take place
in two weeks on November 3rd

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:02.550
with the election.

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:06.100
And so we've organized a
whole series of conversations

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.550
to think about those pressing areas

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:11.283
where scholarship and
action come together.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:14.580
For those of you who
were with us last month,

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:17.140
we started off with Natasha Brown,

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:19.070
who's a voting rights activist

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:20.830
and her work with Black Voters Matter

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:24.470
which is absolutely pivotal
for the moment we're in.

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:27.300
And the ways in which she
actually offered us a new theory

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.880
of thinking about electoral participation

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:33.483
and doing that work she
was literally trans,

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:36.890
holding her conversation with us on a bus,

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:38.960
traveling through North Carolina

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:41.110
going to another voter registration event

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:42.770
was absolutely scintillating

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:45.870
and we'll soon have that
discussion posted online

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:49.630
for you to listen to and think with.

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:52.040
Today, we're going to be
focusing on the politics

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.970
of mass incarceration and health justice

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:58.140
with our guest a physician,
activist Dr. Emily Wang

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:01.474
who Professor Feimster we'll
introduce in just a moment.

00:05:01.474 --> 00:05:03.700
And I just wanna call
attention to the fact

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:06.740
that we have three more events lined up

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:08.770
for the rest of the semester.

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:10.946
You can register for these events

00:05:10.946 --> 00:05:12.963
on our department's website.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:17.800
These events will be held on
November 9th, November 19th,

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:19.460
and December 10th

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:22.450
where we will be continuing
these discussions around

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:25.710
for instance, the role of art as protest.

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:30.710
How art pleads freedom
with the painter activist

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:34.180
Titus Kaphar and with the poet lawyer,

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:37.010
I won't say Laureate, but he's a lawyer.

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:39.130
Reginald Dwayne Betts.

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:41.730
We will have two sessions
on the politics of policing

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:43.210
with our new colleague,

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:45.740
Social Psychologist Phillip Atiba Goff

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:48.590
through his work for the
Center for Policing Equity

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:50.190
that will be on November 19th

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:53.700
and then on December 10th,
another of our new colleagues

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:56.100
Elizabeth Hinton who's a historian

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:59.210
and one of our long time
allies in the law school,

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:01.580
Tracey Meares, we'll be
talking about the work

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:03.660
that's happening at the Yale Law School's

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:04.890
Justice Collaboratory.

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:07.250
So invite everybody who's here today

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:08.710
to sign up for those events,

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:10.800
you can go to our department website

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.263
and register for those Zoom links.

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:17.390
I wanna take a couple more minutes here

00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:20.910
to thank the Collaboratory, so to speak

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:23.373
that's organized and running this series.

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:25.820
Our principal co-sponsor

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:28.350
is the Center for the
Study of Race Indigeneity

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:30.450
and Transnational Migration

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:33.920
and it's director, Stephen
Pitti, Professor Stephen Pitti

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:36.417
and the Assistant Director Matthew Tanico

00:06:36.417 --> 00:06:39.810
had been absolutely
indispensable in helping us

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:41.860
to organize and to mount this series

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:43.480
and I really wanna thank them

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:45.623
for their collaboration and their support.

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:49.490
I wanted to thank the
African-American Studies

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:52.590
Department Senior Administrative
Assistant, Vanessa Epps

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:54.746
who's done a tremendous job

00:06:54.746 --> 00:06:57.089
working with Professor Crystal Feimster

00:06:57.089 --> 00:07:00.430
getting the word out,
getting the series organized.

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:02.630
Thank you so much, Vanessa.

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:07.210
I also wanna thank our tech
support, Emmanuel Bissell.

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:09.860
I hope I'm pronouncing your
name correctly, Emmanuel.

00:07:11.899 --> 00:07:14.380
He's keeping this room up and going,

00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:17.191
so thank you so much, Emmanuel.

00:07:17.191 --> 00:07:21.390
And finally, I want to
thank our chief curator

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:24.840
of this series, Professor
Crystal Feimster.

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:28.130
Now I can let it go
without saying how amazing

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:31.840
and brilliant and wonderful
Professor Feimster is

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:35.840
because many of us here have
the opportunity and the honor

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:40.300
of working with her every
day, taking classes with her

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:42.210
maybe three times a week.

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:44.090
But we have the chance to
work with her everyday,

00:07:44.090 --> 00:07:45.460
but for those of you who don't know her

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:48.650
I just wanna make clear
that Professor Feimster

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:52.510
is one of the nation's
leading historians of USF

00:07:52.510 --> 00:07:55.010
and a black feminist history.

00:07:55.010 --> 00:07:58.130
Her award-winning book
"Southern Whores" transformed

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:01.540
how we understand post
reconstruction era racial violence

00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:06.380
by excavating the
underknown histories of rape

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:09.120
as a tool of racial
domination along with lynching

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:11.000
and those divisive errors.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:13.980
And she's increasing the
magnifying power of that analysis.

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:16.570
In her next book, she's
writing two of them

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:18.990
that examined the uses of sexual violence

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:20.540
in the U.S. Civil War.

00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:22.510
These are gonna be game-changing books

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:24.890
and we're very lucky to have her bringing

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:28.030
that intellectual power
to organizing this series,

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:30.290
so I wanna thank her for her work.

00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:32.440
And with that, I'll turn it over to her

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:33.903
to introduce our guest today.

00:08:35.737 --> 00:08:38.810
- Thank you, Jackie, Professor Goldsby

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:41.300
for that lovely introduction.

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:44.790
And also thank you for making sure

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:49.790
that Endeavors continued this
term under all of the stress

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:53.670
and circumstances that we're all facing.

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:54.927
You were the one who said,

00:08:54.927 --> 00:08:56.540
"We've gotta have these conversations."

00:08:56.540 --> 00:09:01.540
And we in the department and
those in the Yale community

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:07.010
were more than willing to say,
yes to these conversations

00:09:07.990 --> 00:09:10.600
and for all of us to kind of do this work

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:13.500
and be in this space together
in these difficult times

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:14.970
is really important.

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:17.670
So thank you for insisting on that

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:20.620
and leading us in having
these conversations.

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:22.920
I also wanna thank Vanessa.

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:27.920
I just wanna add to your note of gratitude

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:31.520
that Vanessa has sort
of come and new to FAM

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:36.520
and really rallied and
supported me in this series.

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:42.260
So thank you, Vanessa
and Emmanuel as well,

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:45.430
nothing but gratitude
for making this happen.

00:09:45.430 --> 00:09:48.240
It wouldn't be happening
without the two of you,

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:49.970
so thank you.

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:53.130
I also want to thank
in advance racial man,

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:55.090
racial formation folks,

00:09:55.090 --> 00:09:57.870
just because I'm putting you on the spot.

00:09:57.870 --> 00:09:59.110
I know you're here.

00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:02.590
This is part of like
your first year induction

00:10:02.590 --> 00:10:06.880
into African-American studies
and we're glad you're here.

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:09.170
We wanna see you here,
we wanna hear your voice

00:10:09.170 --> 00:10:11.660
and I'm hoping that you're gonna help me

00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:14.960
amplify some of the
things that Professor Wang

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:16.100
is gonna share with us.

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:18.290
So when you hear her saying something

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:20.190
that you think needs to be repeated

00:10:20.190 --> 00:10:21.930
or needs to be highlighted,

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:25.027
I want you to hit that up in the chat

00:10:25.027 --> 00:10:27.440
and we'll repeat some of
the things that she says

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:28.420
and I'll ask questions

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:31.290
and have her go back
over some things for us,

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:32.900
because this is a moment for us

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:36.500
to really learn from Dr. Wang.

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:39.360
And so let's make sure that we highlight

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:41.120
what's important here.

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:45.140
On that note, What's important
is what Professor Wang

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:46.610
is gonna offer up to us today

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:48.770
and I wanna get that conversation going

00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:53.270
and first I wanna introduce
Professor Wang or Dr. Wang.

00:10:53.270 --> 00:10:56.660
So I have to say, I
actually have been sort of,

00:10:56.660 --> 00:10:57.640
I don't wanna say stalking,

00:10:57.640 --> 00:11:01.800
but following Dr. Wang for a while now,

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:04.443
both the professional
and the personal front.

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:07.680
Full disclosure, she
lives in my neighborhood

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:09.710
and she's got three daughters

00:11:09.710 --> 00:11:12.220
who are the same age as my youngest Theia

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:15.960
and they were in Theia's
kindergarten class.

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:19.062
So everybody was like, who's
this person who's got triplets?

00:11:19.062 --> 00:11:20.480
How is she doing this?

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:21.930
And then when I found out who she was,

00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:24.083
I was like, Oh my God, she's amazing.

00:11:25.090 --> 00:11:29.320
And I also first came across
you through your efforts

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:33.840
to Ban the Box, your work
around that and I really was,

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:36.770
it was one of those issues
that I had never thought of

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:39.720
and I remember when you
sent me an email about it,

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:43.350
I was just like, "Who is this
person, what is this issue?"

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:46.590
And so I had kind of
trapped you and followed you

00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:48.950
and I'm so excited that

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:52.400
I get to introduce you to this community

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:55.330
both as like amazing community
person in my neighborhood,

00:11:55.330 --> 00:11:57.500
but also as an amazing scholar.

00:11:57.500 --> 00:12:02.020
So Dr. Wang is an associate professor

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:03.650
in the Yale School of Medicine

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:07.040
and directs the new Center
for Health and Justice

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:09.880
called SEICHE, I think I got it right,

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:12.630
but she will correct me if
I didn't and I'm sure of it.

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:18.680
The Center for Health and
Justice is a collaborative

00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:22.260
between Yale School of
Medicine and Yale Law School

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:25.590
working to stimulate
community transformation,

00:12:25.590 --> 00:12:30.220
identify the legal policy
and practice levers

00:12:30.220 --> 00:12:33.350
that can improve the health
of individuals and communities

00:12:33.350 --> 00:12:35.830
impacted by mass incarceration.

00:12:35.830 --> 00:12:38.920
She also has the Health
Justice Lab that does,

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:40.380
which is a research program

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:43.810
which receives National
Institutes of Health funding

00:12:43.810 --> 00:12:45.459
to investigate how incarceration

00:12:45.459 --> 00:12:48.190
influences chronic health conditions

00:12:48.190 --> 00:12:51.300
including cardiovascular disease, cancer,

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:53.240
and opiod use disorder

00:12:54.417 --> 00:12:58.350
and uses a participatory approach
to studying interventions

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:01.430
which mitigate the
impact of incarceration.

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:03.707
And I wanna just sort of hit that again,

00:13:03.707 --> 00:13:07.410
but she uses a participatory approach

00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:08.840
and I think for those of you

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:11.500
who are coming out of
my civil rights course,

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:14.550
I think this is something I
want you to pay attention to.

00:13:14.550 --> 00:13:16.910
And I want you to think
about some of the people

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:18.160
that we've been talking about,

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:20.870
Ella Baker and
"Participatory of Democracy"

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:25.510
and see what Professor Wang
is gonna tell us about that.

00:13:25.510 --> 00:13:27.270
And finally, she's an internist

00:13:27.270 --> 00:13:29.870
and she has cared for
thousands of individuals

00:13:29.870 --> 00:13:31.900
with a history of incarceration

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:35.520
and is the co-founder of the
Transitions Clinic Network,

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:39.440
a consortium of 40 community
health centers nationwide

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:41.970
dedicated to caring for individuals

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:44.850
recently released from
correctional facilities

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:47.110
by employing community health workers

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:49.170
with histories of incarceration.

00:13:49.170 --> 00:13:52.270
