abolition of the police state

00:15:32.420 --> 00:15:34.980
elaboration of the carceral state.

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:36.810
And the reality that I present

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:40.880
which is that those are all yes and true,

00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:43.270
but I've had the opportunity
as a practicing physician

00:15:43.270 --> 00:15:44.680
as Crystal has indicated

00:15:45.560 --> 00:15:50.100
that you look at examples
right now of closing Rikers

00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:51.143
and if you talk to physicians

00:15:51.143 --> 00:15:53.710
that are practicing within Rikers

00:15:53.710 --> 00:15:57.040
and they've had historic
drops in the jail population

00:15:57.040 --> 00:15:58.080
that we could go into now,

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:03.080
so historic just with stupid, idiot,

00:16:03.180 --> 00:16:05.610
easy changes that should have
long happened but haven't,

00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:06.940
and then they finally did.

00:16:06.940 --> 00:16:09.960
You can get the population
down from 13,000 to 11,000

00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:12.380
to now it's 5,000,

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:13.980
but at the very end of the day,

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:16.200
there's probably about thousands of people

00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:17.600
that won't be able to go home.

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:18.770
There's no place to go home,

00:16:18.770 --> 00:16:20.210
there's no health system to care for them,

00:16:20.210 --> 00:16:21.820
there's no place for them to live.

00:16:21.820 --> 00:16:24.150
Our communities have entirely abrogated

00:16:24.150 --> 00:16:26.690
their responsibilities at
the community health systems,

00:16:26.690 --> 00:16:29.660
the housing systems, et
cetera, to those individuals.

00:16:29.660 --> 00:16:34.660
And so in thinking about
dismantling a carceral state

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:38.110
and thinking about kind of
what abolition looks like

00:16:38.110 --> 00:16:42.000
it requires a real deep reconstruction,

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:47.000
a real reckoning with the
social structures that do exist.

00:16:47.240 --> 00:16:49.400
What does our social contract look like?

00:16:49.400 --> 00:16:52.380
It's so fractured now that
we can't actually provide

00:16:52.380 --> 00:16:53.650
for that space,

00:16:53.650 --> 00:16:55.290
that we can't talk about kind of issues

00:16:55.290 --> 00:16:58.200
of revolution and
abolition without saying,

00:16:58.200 --> 00:17:01.030
that then you need an all
hands on deck approach

00:17:01.030 --> 00:17:03.920
and folks like me within the health system

00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:05.230
have to be on board.

00:17:05.230 --> 00:17:07.540
And so it's in that space that I sit

00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:09.640
and I'm often kind of intention,

00:17:09.640 --> 00:17:12.240
which is the practicality
that every Friday,

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:13.810
Steph knows this, I see patients

00:17:13.810 --> 00:17:16.290
that have just come home
from prison and jail.

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:19.470
And so that keeps it real
because there's a real reality

00:17:19.470 --> 00:17:22.130
and individual with their family sometimes

00:17:22.130 --> 00:17:24.560
and then certainly
returning into the community

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:26.750
where the resources are just limited.

00:17:26.750 --> 00:17:30.240
And it's that space that you
can think really and truly

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:33.210
about what revolution looks
like, it's gonna be a process.

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:34.580
It's a long haul process

00:17:34.580 --> 00:17:36.940
and it needs and requires

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:41.940
a interdisciplinary intersect or approach

00:17:42.030 --> 00:17:44.230
to really thinking about how this looks.

00:17:44.230 --> 00:17:47.000
So I'll start then by saying this,

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:49.800
that increasingly in my work

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:52.720
and Professor Goldsby alluded to this,

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:56.910
what I love most is being in
an interdisciplinary spaces.

00:17:56.910 --> 00:17:59.440
We recently have a grant now actually

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:01.710
that we'll be looking at COVID-19

00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:04.710
and I'll touch on this a little
bit within prisons and jails

00:18:06.170 --> 00:18:09.940
and have an amazing opportunity
also to look at the history

00:18:09.940 --> 00:18:13.390
of mass incarceration and pandemics.

00:18:13.390 --> 00:18:16.230
And so Elizabeth Hinton
will be partnering on this,

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:18.290
we're gonna look at looking
at the legal barriers

00:18:18.290 --> 00:18:20.620
and again, that's Tracey
Meares partnering on this.

00:18:20.620 --> 00:18:22.660
To me, this is where it lies

00:18:22.660 --> 00:18:25.546
is that if we do not have scholars

00:18:25.546 --> 00:18:28.970
and also people that
have been incarcerated

00:18:28.970 --> 00:18:31.890
and also people that are
currently incarcerated,

00:18:31.890 --> 00:18:34.640
knee-deep in the solutions of the past,

00:18:34.640 --> 00:18:36.700
knee-deep in what has worked in the past

00:18:36.700 --> 00:18:38.650
and what just didn't get scaled up

00:18:38.650 --> 00:18:41.280
and knee-deep on what we
are presented with now,

00:18:41.280 --> 00:18:42.890
we're just not gonna get anywhere.

00:18:42.890 --> 00:18:47.163
And so it's that place too
that I really like to be.

