00:19:55.849 --> 00:19:59.440
- One of the places I'll
start part is just to share

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:01.237
that oftentimes people have,

00:20:01.237 --> 00:20:03.700
and I think the media presents it poorly,

00:20:03.700 --> 00:20:05.960
which is that the folks
who are incarcerated

00:20:05.960 --> 00:20:07.704
that's 2.2 million individuals

00:20:07.704 --> 00:20:10.083
and then we have a whole system
of community supervision,

00:20:10.083 --> 00:20:12.620
but 2.2 million individuals
that are incarcerated

00:20:12.620 --> 00:20:15.930
are this like young, healthy
lot that's able to exercise.

00:20:15.930 --> 00:20:18.730
They're doing well, they
did a crime, they're inside

00:20:18.730 --> 00:20:21.630
and the truth is that
that just isn't the case.

00:20:21.630 --> 00:20:23.620
So the patient demographic,

00:20:23.620 --> 00:20:25.970
and I frequently use the term patients,

00:20:25.970 --> 00:20:27.960
they're not all patients,
but that is just my lens,

00:20:27.960 --> 00:20:29.290
so I apologize for that.

00:20:29.290 --> 00:20:32.710
But the patient demographic is changing.

00:20:32.710 --> 00:20:33.960
The population behind bars,

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:36.410
given the incredibly
long sentences we've had

00:20:36.410 --> 00:20:39.200
have meant that there's a
disproportionate proportion

00:20:39.200 --> 00:20:41.643
of individuals that are 55 and older.

00:20:43.935 --> 00:20:48.360
And when the last
estimates were looked at,

00:20:48.360 --> 00:20:51.960
it's about 80% of individuals
within jails and prisons

00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:54.060
that have a chronic health condition

00:20:54.060 --> 00:20:56.270
that warrants longitudinal primary care.

00:20:56.270 --> 00:20:59.374
So this includes like this
patient here who he's got COPD

00:20:59.374 --> 00:21:01.650
and I shared these pictures just for you

00:21:01.650 --> 00:21:04.400
to give a look at what
healthcare looks like.

00:21:04.400 --> 00:21:05.770
They're waiting to see a doctor,

00:21:05.770 --> 00:21:06.810
so there's one patient here

00:21:06.810 --> 00:21:08.360
that's getting kind of an albuterol

00:21:08.360 --> 00:21:10.810
like a treatment to help open his lungs

00:21:10.810 --> 00:21:13.520
next to another patient
who has a mental illness

00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:15.030
and he's in a cage.

00:21:15.030 --> 00:21:18.273
So this is how healthcare
is structured behind bars.

00:21:19.130 --> 00:21:21.530
And so when I say that about 80%

00:21:21.530 --> 00:21:24.483
have a chronic health
condition, so for a range of,

00:21:25.350 --> 00:21:26.810
what I mean is a range of conditions.

00:21:26.810 --> 00:21:31.810
So high blood pressure,
asthma, COPD like this guy has

00:21:31.850 --> 00:21:33.570
to also hepatitis C,

00:21:33.570 --> 00:21:37.030
infectious diseases
like HIV and hepatitis C

00:21:37.030 --> 00:21:39.840
to also depression,
post-traumatic stress disorder

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:41.650
schizophrenia, opioid use disorder,

00:21:41.650 --> 00:21:44.970
all those conditions require, I'd say,

00:21:44.970 --> 00:21:47.460
most physicians would say
a primary care doctor.

00:21:47.460 --> 00:21:49.980
And so while we have the impressions

00:21:49.980 --> 00:21:53.010
that folks are generally
healthy, in fact, they aren't

00:21:53.010 --> 00:21:54.720
and they're aging behind bars

00:21:54.720 --> 00:21:57.220
in systems that were never designed

00:21:57.220 --> 00:21:58.590
to actually provide the health care

00:21:58.590 --> 00:22:00.090
for chronic health conditions.

00:22:03.380 --> 00:22:04.380
- Emily.

00:22:04.380 --> 00:22:05.540
- Yeah.

00:22:05.540 --> 00:22:09.625
- I'm curious, I'm thinking
about a few years ago,

00:22:09.625 --> 00:22:13.230
there were black women in Alabama,

00:22:13.230 --> 00:22:15.900
two sisters I think who
had like kidney failure.

00:22:15.900 --> 00:22:18.430
I don't know if you remember this story,

00:22:18.430 --> 00:22:20.877
but it seems to me that there was a moment

00:22:20.877 --> 00:22:22.930
and I don't know if it's still the case

00:22:22.930 --> 00:22:24.860
that around sort of decarceration

00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:31.580
that was linked to the
economics of healthcare, right?

00:22:32.100 --> 00:22:33.863
That in some of these States,

00:22:35.360 --> 00:22:37.770
the Republicans and
more conservative people

00:22:37.770 --> 00:22:42.060
were willing to think about decarceration

00:22:42.060 --> 00:22:45.430
and reducing the number of
people who are incarcerated

00:22:45.430 --> 00:22:48.160
because of health expenses

00:22:48.160 --> 00:22:51.073
and the sort of cost of healthcare.

00:22:52.245 --> 00:22:56.622
Is that something that has
happened or is happening?

00:22:56.622 --> 00:23:00.523
- Yeah, that's a really
important point, so yes, it is.

00:23:02.040 --> 00:23:04.150
I've been doing this for over a decade

00:23:04.150 --> 00:23:05.380
and I'd say right now,

00:23:05.380 --> 00:23:08.460
we're at a place of
increasing bipartisan support

00:23:08.460 --> 00:23:10.530
that kind of whatever you call this.

00:23:10.530 --> 00:23:12.820
The Carceral State Mass Incarceration

00:23:12.820 --> 00:23:13.800
has been a failed policy

00:23:13.800 --> 00:23:16.230
it hasn't changed violent crime rates

00:23:16.230 --> 00:23:18.520
and also costs us too much.

00:23:18.520 --> 00:23:20.590
So you'll see more right-leaning folks

00:23:20.590 --> 00:23:23.000
or just blatantly right folks saying,

00:23:23.000 --> 00:23:25.210
there's a whole right on crime, right?

00:23:25.210 --> 00:23:27.720
That it's the costs of incarceration

00:23:27.720 --> 00:23:29.463
that are what are driving,

00:23:30.500 --> 00:23:33.210
is there animus for trying
to decarcerate our country.

00:23:33.210 --> 00:23:35.170
But there is consensus

00:23:35.170 --> 00:23:38.300
that the prison and jail
population is too big

00:23:38.300 --> 00:23:40.700
and in large part these costs are driven

00:23:40.700 --> 00:23:43.270
by healthcare costs, exactly
as you're pointing out

00:23:43.270 --> 00:23:45.930
the aging your two sisters
with end-stage renal disease,

00:23:45.930 --> 00:23:48.940
they cost and they cost
more in corrections

00:23:48.940 --> 00:23:50.810
than they would in the community.

00:23:50.810 --> 00:23:52.603
- [Crystal] Right, okay, thank you.

00:23:55.757 --> 00:23:59.210
- And then, of course, and
especially thinking about COVID,

00:23:59.210 --> 00:24:00.670
which we could talk about a little bit

00:24:00.670 --> 00:24:02.670
because maybe that's my
work in progress right now

00:24:02.670 --> 00:24:03.670
that we can touch on

00:24:04.750 --> 00:24:05.740
that prison environments

00:24:05.740 --> 00:24:07.390
can, of course, exacerbate poor health.

00:24:07.390 --> 00:24:08.900
And I just want you to
think about the world

00:24:08.900 --> 00:24:10.780
in which we're living right now, right?

00:24:10.780 --> 00:24:13.100
We're masked, socially distance,

00:24:13.100 --> 00:24:16.780
and think about these
individuals right here.

00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:18.510
So all these pictures, I should say

00:24:18.510 --> 00:24:20.760
come from California
Department of Corrections.

00:24:20.760 --> 00:24:24.470
I actually did my residency
training in San Francisco

00:24:24.470 --> 00:24:26.630
and had a longitudinal primary care clinic

00:24:26.630 --> 00:24:28.040
within this prison in San Quentin.

00:24:28.040 --> 00:24:31.790
And so, these pictures come from them

00:24:31.790 --> 00:24:34.174
and I just use it to highlight

00:24:34.174 --> 00:24:36.660
that it's incredibly overcrowded.

00:24:36.660 --> 00:24:38.630
Again, our policies of mass incarceration

00:24:38.630 --> 00:24:40.900
have led to situations like this,

00:24:40.900 --> 00:24:43.110
where people are triple
bunking and gymnasiums

00:24:43.110 --> 00:24:45.480
and then, of course,
in these circumstances

00:24:45.480 --> 00:24:47.660
are hotbeds for COVID-19.

00:24:47.660 --> 00:24:49.980
We can go into that for forever,
but you can only imagine

00:24:49.980 --> 00:24:52.680
how you just can't socially
distance in places like this.

00:24:52.680 --> 00:24:55.310
But also places where,
because of the stress

00:24:55.310 --> 00:24:56.760
rates of cardiovascular disease,

00:24:56.760 --> 00:24:58.940
we're hypothesizing are increasing.

00:24:58.940 --> 00:25:01.110
So, there's a lot of work thinking about

00:25:01.110 --> 00:25:03.210
kind of toxic stress
and what this looks like

00:25:03.210 --> 00:25:08.210
and amped up stress responses

00:25:08.220 --> 00:25:11.980
and in particular, what that
means for short-term health,

00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:12.843
but also long-term health,

00:25:12.843 --> 00:25:15.080
what that means for mental
health, but also physical health.

00:25:15.080 --> 00:25:17.810
And so the actual environment,
they built environment,

00:25:17.810 --> 00:25:20.330
but also the ways that
prisons and jails are defined

00:25:20.330 --> 00:25:23.563
really kind of can magnify
different conditions in health.

00:25:27.610 --> 00:25:30.540
And so what drew me to this is as follows,

00:25:30.540 --> 00:25:32.390
so this blew my mind.

00:25:32.390 --> 00:25:35.030
It's like I don't think
I would have ever been

00:25:35.030 --> 00:25:37.710
like I was a primary care
doctor, thinking about medicine,

00:25:37.710 --> 00:25:40.370
and I wouldn't have seen, if
you asked me 20 years ago,

00:25:40.370 --> 00:25:42.970
would this be kind of
the focus of what I did?

00:25:42.970 --> 00:25:47.970
It wouldn't have been, but prior
to the Affordable Care Act,

00:25:47.980 --> 00:25:51.460
prisons and jails are one of
the only places in the U.S.

00:25:51.460 --> 00:25:54.170
where healthcare is
constitutionally guaranteed.

00:25:54.170 --> 00:25:59.170
And so what this meant for me,
it kind of is inconceivable,

00:26:00.150 --> 00:26:01.940
but you'll see why this matters a lot.

00:26:01.940 --> 00:26:04.650
There was a court case
in 1976 Estelle V Gamble

00:26:04.650 --> 00:26:06.990
that kind of created the
constitutional rights

00:26:06.990 --> 00:26:09.090
to healthcare behind bars.

00:26:09.090 --> 00:26:11.130
Is that if you think about a population

00:26:11.130 --> 00:26:13.920
that's 2.2 million people
that are incarcerated

00:26:13.920 --> 00:26:16.530
who are disproportionately Black, Latino,

00:26:16.530 --> 00:26:20.830
and a racial and ethnic
background of low income,

00:26:20.830 --> 00:26:24.390
oftentimes, prisons and
jails are the first place

00:26:24.390 --> 00:26:27.430
where individuals access
healthcare as adults.

00:26:27.430 --> 00:26:30.300
This is where they learn,
this is where they experience,

00:26:30.300 --> 00:26:32.080
this is where they meet
their first doctor,

00:26:32.080 --> 00:26:34.680
this is where they understand
their chronic conditions,

00:26:34.680 --> 00:26:37.310
and so this has significant bearing

00:26:37.310 --> 00:26:41.150
for their kind of relationship
with the healthcare system,

00:26:41.150 --> 00:26:43.130
their trust of the system.

00:26:43.130 --> 00:26:45.590
You can imagine now in
COVID-19, their trust

00:26:45.590 --> 00:26:49.180
and participating in any sort of issue

00:26:49.180 --> 00:26:53.450
that is tied to healthcare,
healthcare system design.

00:26:53.450 --> 00:26:56.940
And to me it also speaks to this,

00:26:56.940 --> 00:27:00.399
which is that it has been a
place as you hear me go through

00:27:00.399 --> 00:27:03.260
a place where people are first accessing

00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:04.690
preventive health measures.

00:27:04.690 --> 00:27:06.630
Actually do get treated for the first time

00:27:06.630 --> 00:27:08.970
for hepatitis C in certain circumstances.

00:27:08.970 --> 00:27:12.420
And so it's a conflicted
place personally I think

00:27:12.420 --> 00:27:15.180
because we do such a
terrible job in the community

00:27:15.180 --> 00:27:18.200
and because we have so many
Black and Brown people inside

00:27:18.200 --> 00:27:23.200
that there is this perverse
kind of experience of healthcare

00:27:23.320 --> 00:27:25.270
that starts in prisons and jails

00:27:25.270 --> 00:27:27.180
and then gets transitioned home.

00:27:27.180 --> 00:27:28.700
So if you look at our data and others,

00:27:28.700 --> 00:27:31.200
about 40% of individuals
that are incarcerated

00:27:31.200 --> 00:27:33.120
are newly diagnosed
with a chronic condition

00:27:33.120 --> 00:27:34.470
while they're incarcerated.

00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:41.720
- Emily, so since so many
young Black and Brown folks

00:27:45.390 --> 00:27:48.320
are having for the first time
interacting with doctors,

00:27:48.320 --> 00:27:52.970
I'm curious if you could
speak to who are the doctors,

00:27:52.970 --> 00:27:57.470
what kind of doctors are
doing this kind of service?

00:27:58.600 --> 00:27:59.720
Who are the doctors?

00:27:59.720 --> 00:28:03.260
- You know, I actually think
Crystal it's like a mixed bag.

00:28:03.260 --> 00:28:04.470
So in the news you're gonna hear,

00:28:04.470 --> 00:28:07.830
it's like the problematic stories.

00:28:07.830 --> 00:28:10.500
So people that are not
boarded in their specialty,

00:28:10.500 --> 00:28:13.810
oftentimes it's like
misbehaving physicians

00:28:13.810 --> 00:28:16.080
or criminal physicians, frankly

00:28:16.080 --> 00:28:18.820
that have lost licensure, then
moved to a different state,

00:28:18.820 --> 00:28:21.200
then have started a practice
within a correctional system,

00:28:21.200 --> 00:28:23.550
and doing stuff out of licensure.

00:28:23.550 --> 00:28:25.990
And also I would say that
there are also physicians

00:28:25.990 --> 00:28:28.010
that commit to it because
they're kind of interested

00:28:28.010 --> 00:28:31.330
in taking care of patients
that are more vulnerable,

00:28:31.330 --> 00:28:32.850
really trying to think about this as,

00:28:32.850 --> 00:28:35.940
and I'd say that's the younger
generation of physicians

00:28:35.940 --> 00:28:37.000
that are more embracing

00:28:37.000 --> 00:28:40.050
of kind of a more social justice vision,

00:28:40.050 --> 00:28:41.710
but it's a mixed bag.

00:28:41.710 --> 00:28:44.170
I also think you're
going to a place where,

00:28:44.170 --> 00:28:46.300
if you think about
community health systems

00:28:46.300 --> 00:28:48.810
there is an infrastructure
that protects you.

00:28:48.810 --> 00:28:51.620
So, you Crystal when
you go to the hospital

00:28:51.620 --> 00:28:54.890
and you're seeing a doctor
Yale New Haven Hospital,

00:28:54.890 --> 00:28:56.160
you can rest assured

00:28:56.160 --> 00:28:58.360
that there are certain kind of approvals

00:28:58.360 --> 00:28:59.840
that have to be taken on

00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:02.480
in order for that
hospital to get financed.

00:29:02.480 --> 00:29:05.360
These are national guidelines
with a certain quality of care

00:29:05.360 --> 00:29:07.640
that has to be delivered
in the health system,

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:09.250
otherwise they don't get the funding

00:29:09.250 --> 00:29:12.220
from Medicaid, Medicare,
and private insurers.

00:29:12.220 --> 00:29:14.630
Okay, so you can be certain of it.

00:29:14.630 --> 00:29:17.830
There is no federal or
state mandatory oversight

00:29:17.830 --> 00:29:22.000
of healthcare behind bars, period, none.

00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:24.860
And so again, there's a heterogeneity,

00:29:24.860 --> 00:29:27.470
so I don't wanna impugn colleagues of mine

00:29:27.470 --> 00:29:29.740
that work inside doing the good work

00:29:29.740 --> 00:29:33.097
and also you just don't know
what the work is, right?

00:29:33.097 --> 00:29:35.410
It's like we have no way of knowing.

00:29:35.410 --> 00:29:37.830
So when you go into
Yale New Haven Hospital,

00:29:37.830 --> 00:29:40.580
you can rest assured that
it's been approved by Jayco.

00:29:40.580 --> 00:29:43.460
When you go in here, there's
just no way of knowing.

00:29:43.460 --> 00:29:45.660
And this is inherently the issue

00:29:45.660 --> 00:29:48.760
which is that there's no
transparency, there's no kind of,

00:29:48.760 --> 00:29:51.820
our taxpayer cash pays for
the care that's delivered

00:29:51.820 --> 00:29:53.330
in Connecticut Department of Corrections

00:29:53.330 --> 00:29:55.750
and you have no idea of knowing

00:29:55.750 --> 00:29:58.600
if they're meeting the
standard metrics for quality,

00:29:58.600 --> 00:30:01.000
nor do you know about
the quality positions.

00:30:01.000 --> 00:30:02.950
- [Crystal] All right, okay, thank you.

00:30:05.197 --> 00:30:07.210
- And so you know, I just highlight this

00:30:07.210 --> 00:30:11.300
and maybe this photo is
better for physicians

00:30:11.300 --> 00:30:14.530
and training our students

00:30:14.530 --> 00:30:15.730
in the health allied professionals.

00:30:15.730 --> 00:30:17.130
But you know again,

00:30:17.130 --> 00:30:20.470
while there is this
constitutional access to care

00:30:20.470 --> 00:30:23.550
it's just totally entirely different.

00:30:23.550 --> 00:30:25.800
And so this was a patient that I had met.

00:30:25.800 --> 00:30:28.950
He had just been diagnosed
with like a condition

00:30:28.950 --> 00:30:30.640
where your blood pressure
is out of control,

00:30:30.640 --> 00:30:33.080
it's so crazy that you
might be at risk for stroke.

00:30:33.080 --> 00:30:34.250
It's called hypertensive emergency.

00:30:34.250 --> 00:30:36.240
So we sent out to outside hospitals,

00:30:36.240 --> 00:30:38.140
so they have to contract out, of course,

00:30:38.140 --> 00:30:40.370
'cause most prisons don't
have their own hospital,

00:30:40.370 --> 00:30:42.830
comes back in and this is
supposed to be the place

00:30:42.830 --> 00:30:45.270
where he recuperates, okay?

00:30:45.270 --> 00:30:48.234
So just to start, just look around,

00:30:48.234 --> 00:30:51.330
I'm rounding as a physician

00:30:51.330 --> 00:30:53.590
and everyone in earshot
correction officers there

00:30:53.590 --> 00:30:56.240
that has the person right next to him

00:30:56.240 --> 00:30:58.260
incarcerated as well as listening,

00:30:58.260 --> 00:30:59.910
you throw the cuff through,

00:30:59.910 --> 00:31:01.490
many of you probably have gone to a doctor

00:31:01.490 --> 00:31:03.090
where they check a blood pressure.

00:31:03.090 --> 00:31:05.060
This is not properly positioned,

00:31:05.060 --> 00:31:06.510
he has to place it on yourselves

00:31:06.510 --> 00:31:08.730
and you're kind of having a
DL conversation with them,

00:31:08.730 --> 00:31:11.030
trying to see, what did you learn.

00:31:11.030 --> 00:31:13.200
If you're the doctor in
there and there I was,

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:14.410
of course, there's drugs in there

00:31:14.410 --> 00:31:16.580
and you wanna talk to them about things

00:31:16.580 --> 00:31:20.070
that doctors would talk
about and you're not able to.

00:31:20.070 --> 00:31:22.080
The other thing to just note is that

00:31:23.160 --> 00:31:25.240
this patient will never
have to call a pharmacy

00:31:25.240 --> 00:31:28.230
to get his meds, never even
have to give himself a reminder,

00:31:28.230 --> 00:31:31.100
like the whole kind of how he
manages his chronic condition

00:31:31.100 --> 00:31:33.560
for those of you are on
blood pressure pills.

00:31:33.560 --> 00:31:35.660
What happens is that
every morning he's called

00:31:35.660 --> 00:31:38.820
at the crack of dawn,
correctional officer wakes him up,

00:31:38.820 --> 00:31:41.960
he goes and makes a line, his
cup is filled, it's a Medline,

00:31:41.960 --> 00:31:44.130
he has to drink it

00:31:44.130 --> 00:31:47.460
and then the nurse checks
to see if he's cheeked it

00:31:47.460 --> 00:31:50.800
and then come and then he
makes it back to his room.

00:31:50.800 --> 00:31:53.720
And so it's incredibly passive.

00:31:53.720 --> 00:31:57.503
It's almost, but totally
paternalistic like it's childlike,

00:31:58.400 --> 00:32:00.177
but adherence is 100%.

00:32:01.740 --> 00:32:05.601
If you donate here, we've done
studies that have shown this.

00:32:05.601 --> 00:32:07.220
For instance, let's say you're like

00:32:07.220 --> 00:32:10.383
I'm not feeling like getting
an insulin injection today,

00:32:13.330 --> 00:32:14.660
for whatever reason,

00:32:14.660 --> 00:32:18.610
folks participants have stated in studies

00:32:18.610 --> 00:32:20.460
that they've been sent
us all to a confinement.

00:32:20.460 --> 00:32:24.410
And so there is this
entirely different notion

00:32:24.410 --> 00:32:27.080
of how healthcare is delivered,
how it's administered,

00:32:27.080 --> 00:32:30.290
how people see it, and how
people are punished for it,

00:32:30.290 --> 00:32:32.740
within corrections that, of
course, doesn't translate

00:32:32.740 --> 00:32:35.730
into the types of a
doctor-patient relationship

00:32:35.730 --> 00:32:36.823
you wanna create.

00:32:40.300 --> 00:32:41.956
The other thing too is that every time

00:32:41.956 --> 00:32:44.260
this patient wants to see a doctor.

00:32:44.260 --> 00:32:46.680
So in Connecticut, for instance,

00:32:46.680 --> 00:32:50.450
for a chronic disease visit,
you have to pay three bucks.

00:32:50.450 --> 00:32:53.170
So you're like, wow,
what's three bucks, right?

00:32:53.170 --> 00:32:55.710
Well, three bucks is
equivalent to four days work

00:32:55.710 --> 00:32:57.210
if you're lucky enough to have a job.

00:32:57.210 --> 00:32:58.310
And so again,

00:32:58.310 --> 00:33:01.960
there's just so many
different barriers that exist

00:33:01.960 --> 00:33:04.810
for people that are incarcerated
to actually access care.

00:33:06.810 --> 00:33:07.900
This picture I highlight,

00:33:07.900 --> 00:33:10.960
so it's just pointing out this pink slip.

00:33:10.960 --> 00:33:14.710
And so it's a kite that
patients have to fill out again,

00:33:14.710 --> 00:33:17.900
when they wanna see a provider,

00:33:17.900 --> 00:33:19.040
healthcare provider or doctor.

00:33:19.040 --> 00:33:22.820
And so again, this is no
longer happening in California,

00:33:22.820 --> 00:33:25.170
but it's still happening
all over the country.

00:33:25.170 --> 00:33:26.870
The person fills it out

00:33:26.870 --> 00:33:30.240
that then gets evaluated
by a correctional officer.

00:33:30.240 --> 00:33:32.530
The correctional officer then decides

00:33:32.530 --> 00:33:34.570
whether or not it's a
valid medical concern,

00:33:34.570 --> 00:33:37.210
it goes to a nurse and then
it goes to a physician.

00:33:37.210 --> 00:33:42.080
And so again, all sorts of
barriers to getting care.

00:33:42.080 --> 00:33:44.530
This also is an HIV clinic

00:33:44.530 --> 00:33:47.910
and so again, there's
just no confidentiality

00:33:47.910 --> 00:33:49.970
again, there's a correctional
officer walking by.

00:33:49.970 --> 00:33:52.320
Everyone in this line knows

00:33:52.320 --> 00:33:53.917
that they're all going
to see the HIV doctor.

00:33:53.917 --> 00:33:56.650
And so just the whole
notion that kind of values

00:33:56.650 --> 00:33:58.610
that we hold true and are principled

00:33:58.610 --> 00:34:01.120
in one's around human dignity and rights

00:34:02.010 --> 00:34:05.760
are infringed upon and
infringed upon largely

00:34:05.760 --> 00:34:10.760
a population that is of low
income and racial minorities

00:34:12.140 --> 00:34:15.303
that again have rarely accessed
the community health system.

00:34:17.180 --> 00:34:19.540
And then, I always show this picture

00:34:19.540 --> 00:34:23.750
and for me this is just
the deep brutality,

00:34:23.750 --> 00:34:25.860
the kind of the violence

00:34:25.860 --> 00:34:30.860
that the health system
can exert on individuals.

00:34:30.870 --> 00:34:34.050
And so, think about that first
patient that I showed you

00:34:34.050 --> 00:34:36.647
the older gentleman that maybe
it's requiring oxygen now,

00:34:36.647 --> 00:34:38.530
and this happened to be not the same guy,

00:34:38.530 --> 00:34:43.530
but another patient who needs
chronic oxygen administration

00:34:43.740 --> 00:34:46.000
for his COPD.

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:50.330
And you can just note here
that the oxygen's outside

00:34:50.330 --> 00:34:52.080
his solitary confinement door.

00:34:52.080 --> 00:34:54.587
And so, you would hear
him is when it changed

00:34:54.587 --> 00:34:56.720
like bamming on the door.

00:34:56.720 --> 00:34:59.330
And you'd walk by and
doctors would walk by

00:34:59.330 --> 00:35:00.650
and I remember being a person in-training

00:35:00.650 --> 00:35:02.970
being like, how's this permissible.

00:35:02.970 --> 00:35:06.375
And it's because the values of safety,

00:35:06.375 --> 00:35:11.375
really trump the values of
dignity, but of health, right?

00:35:12.030 --> 00:35:17.030
And so there's many, many
different expressions of violence

00:35:17.120 --> 00:35:20.470
that are just ingrained within the system

00:35:20.470 --> 00:35:23.100
and are difficult to, of course, shake off

00:35:23.100 --> 00:35:24.330
when people come home.

00:35:24.330 --> 00:35:26.380
You wonder why there's
all sorts of notions

00:35:26.380 --> 00:35:28.860
within medicine about noncompliance

00:35:28.860 --> 00:35:31.370
and people that are
mistrustful and these people.

00:35:31.370 --> 00:35:36.210
And then you think about this
and that's life preserving

00:35:36.210 --> 00:35:37.540
to actually be mistrustful.

00:35:37.540 --> 00:35:41.020
It's life preserving to not
kind of trust in health systems.

00:35:41.020 --> 00:35:42.840
It literally is the smartest

00:35:42.840 --> 00:35:44.663
and strategic thing that you can do.

00:35:47.717 --> 00:35:51.340
And so, where my work kind of sits mostly

00:35:51.340 --> 00:35:54.470
at this intersection, I'd say,
is that the vast majority of

00:35:54.470 --> 00:35:56.840
you know, Jeremy Travis
said that nearly 95%

00:35:56.840 --> 00:35:58.600
of individuals come home to the community

00:35:58.600 --> 00:35:59.540
and that's about rights.

00:35:59.540 --> 00:36:02.740
That for what it is,
we have 2.2 million in

00:36:02.740 --> 00:36:06.410
and 95% folks will actually
end up coming home.

00:36:06.410 --> 00:36:10.480
And so I think what is
the most interesting to me

00:36:10.480 --> 00:36:14.080
is that it's actually
their health outcomes.

00:36:14.080 --> 00:36:15.910
You know, if you compare what happens

00:36:15.910 --> 00:36:18.690
when they're incarcerated
versus when they come home

00:36:18.690 --> 00:36:22.580
are significantly worse
when people come home.

00:36:22.580 --> 00:36:25.090
So you have about a 12 times
increased risk of dying

00:36:25.090 --> 00:36:28.120
in the first two weeks, post release.

00:36:28.120 --> 00:36:30.020
Big seminal study published

00:36:30.020 --> 00:36:32.340
in New England Journal
of Medicine that showed

00:36:32.340 --> 00:36:37.300
that the first most common
cause of dying is overdose,

00:36:37.300 --> 00:36:40.740
but then heart disease,
homicide specifically.

00:36:43.328 --> 00:36:45.660
Okay, there's homicide,
suicide, and cancer

00:36:45.660 --> 00:36:47.540
and then other chronic conditions as well

00:36:47.540 --> 00:36:51.130
and depending on what States
data you're looking at,

00:36:51.130 --> 00:36:52.697
it's like hepatitis C cancer is from that.

00:36:52.697 --> 00:36:55.860
But again, and it really
elevated risk of dying

00:36:55.860 --> 00:36:57.840
in this first two weeks, post-release

00:36:57.840 --> 00:37:00.770
a really elevated risk of hospitalizations

00:37:00.770 --> 00:37:04.270
and not surprisingly, a worsening
of all chronic conditions.

00:37:04.270 --> 00:37:08.400
And this is because we
have just so many barriers

00:37:08.400 --> 00:37:09.750
to people coming home, right?

00:37:09.750 --> 00:37:14.290
So inabilities to, as crystal mentioned,

00:37:14.290 --> 00:37:16.760
you can't find a job because
you always have to check a box.

00:37:16.760 --> 00:37:18.190
You cannot find a place to live,

00:37:18.190 --> 00:37:19.400
there's a ban on food stamps.

00:37:19.400 --> 00:37:21.810
You cannot find a place to live

00:37:21.810 --> 00:37:23.660
because you you have a criminal record

00:37:23.660 --> 00:37:24.880
and you can't live in public housing

00:37:24.880 --> 00:37:26.430
or your family can't house you

00:37:26.430 --> 00:37:27.480
because you have a criminal record

00:37:27.480 --> 00:37:28.930
and it just goes on and on.

00:37:28.930 --> 00:37:32.130
And so the real challenge
for us as a health system

00:37:32.130 --> 00:37:36.370
is figuring out how better to
engage people who come home.

00:37:36.370 --> 00:37:39.030
And so one of the things
that Crystal alludes too,

00:37:39.030 --> 00:37:41.540
and I won't go into it much now,

00:37:41.540 --> 00:37:45.020
but it's just that we are
using participatory approaches

00:37:45.020 --> 00:37:47.540
about now 15 years ago

00:37:47.540 --> 00:37:50.410
started a transitions clinic
program in San Francisco.

00:37:50.410 --> 00:37:53.260
It's where I trained and really deeply

00:37:53.260 --> 00:37:54.300
in partnership with people

00:37:54.300 --> 00:37:56.210
that had been previously incarcerated.

00:37:56.210 --> 00:37:59.620
There's a long civil rights
movement that is embedded there

00:37:59.620 --> 00:38:01.850
that we partnered with all of us or none

00:38:05.355 --> 00:38:07.680
and really created a program

00:38:07.680 --> 00:38:10.910
that I think was centered
around the values,

00:38:10.910 --> 00:38:12.290
preferences, and needs of people

00:38:12.290 --> 00:38:13.880
with histories of incarceration.

00:38:13.880 --> 00:38:16.650
And core among that,
and here's the picture

00:38:16.650 --> 00:38:19.490
here on the right who's our
first community health worker

00:38:19.490 --> 00:38:20.650
health community health workers

00:38:20.650 --> 00:38:22.510
that are embedded within
primary care clinics

00:38:22.510 --> 00:38:25.560
that have histories of
incarceration themselves.

00:38:25.560 --> 00:38:30.560
And really, I think part of
what I've in doing this work,

00:38:31.440 --> 00:38:33.433
I personally haven't been incarcerated.

00:38:36.152 --> 00:38:37.740
Wen we started this program,

00:38:37.740 --> 00:38:40.840
I'll say now we have like 45
programs all across the country

00:38:40.840 --> 00:38:43.290
there are so many deep naysayers

00:38:43.290 --> 00:38:45.480
like our whole science, our whole notion,

00:38:45.480 --> 00:38:47.250
especially within health systems

00:38:47.250 --> 00:38:48.840
is one about mitigating risks.

00:38:48.840 --> 00:38:51.410
It's like all this stuff
is wrong in these people

00:38:51.410 --> 00:38:53.580
and we just have to make
a more normal, right,

00:38:53.580 --> 00:38:54.830
that there isn't a lot of value

00:38:54.830 --> 00:38:59.830
and what we're really
trying to do as a community,

00:38:59.840 --> 00:39:02.320
within transitions is
to change that narrative

00:39:02.320 --> 00:39:04.540
is there's so much going right.

00:39:04.540 --> 00:39:08.080
You know, there are lots of people

00:39:08.080 --> 00:39:11.330
and assets within communities

00:39:11.330 --> 00:39:13.210
that have been impacted
by mass incarceration

00:39:13.210 --> 00:39:16.300
and that too should inform our science.

00:39:16.300 --> 00:39:18.700
And so, through the years,
what we've been able to do

00:39:18.700 --> 00:39:22.990
is using kind of the
best of impaired design,

00:39:22.990 --> 00:39:25.240
using randomized controlled trials

00:39:25.240 --> 00:39:27.790
show that Transitions Clinic Programs

