this study that we did

00:40:47.570 --> 00:40:50.050
so on the left-hand column I mean,

00:40:50.050 --> 00:40:51.460
'cause probably don't recognize,

00:40:51.460 --> 00:40:53.410
but these are large cohort studies.

00:40:53.410 --> 00:40:56.740
So population-based studies
where they follow thousands,

00:40:56.740 --> 00:40:59.550
sometimes tens of thousands
of individuals in the U.S.

00:40:59.550 --> 00:41:03.540
that are really keyed on,
they're funded by the NIH,

00:41:03.540 --> 00:41:05.900
the National Institutes of
Health, so taxpayer dollars

00:41:05.900 --> 00:41:09.200
trying to figure out the core
etiology of heart disease.

00:41:09.200 --> 00:41:10.470
Okay, so these are all the big ones

00:41:10.470 --> 00:41:14.090
like Cardia, Jackson
Heart Study, Mesa, ENRICH,

00:41:14.090 --> 00:41:15.330
you see them all there, okay.

00:41:15.330 --> 00:41:18.060
And so we took all the
studies that follow people

00:41:18.060 --> 00:41:20.183
for over two years, okay.

00:41:22.004 --> 00:41:25.280
And we're intentionally designed

00:41:25.280 --> 00:41:28.740
to study what are black-white
differences, okay.

00:41:28.740 --> 00:41:32.733
And what we wanted to do was in particular

00:41:32.733 --> 00:41:35.160
to how much mass incarceration

00:41:35.160 --> 00:41:38.180
has impacted the validity
of these studies.

00:41:38.180 --> 00:41:41.480
Okay, so we know that
based on earlier work,

00:41:41.480 --> 00:41:44.460
there's about a one in three
lifetime risk of a black man

00:41:44.460 --> 00:41:47.420
being incarcerated in this
country, over his lifetime.

00:41:47.420 --> 00:41:48.960
Okay, and it's even higher,

00:41:48.960 --> 00:41:51.590
the risk is higher in certain communities,

00:41:51.590 --> 00:41:56.360
but it's one in three
population-based over in this country.

00:41:56.360 --> 00:41:59.670
And so one of the questions
we wanted to know was

00:41:59.670 --> 00:42:02.490
how often do these big population studies

00:42:02.490 --> 00:42:04.060
actually go into prisons and jails?

00:42:04.060 --> 00:42:07.260
How often do they include the
folks that are incarcerated?

00:42:07.260 --> 00:42:10.430
And then let's say that
you recruit someone

00:42:10.430 --> 00:42:13.080
in the community, a
young healthy black man

00:42:13.080 --> 00:42:14.660
and then he gets picked up by the system,

00:42:14.660 --> 00:42:15.880
do they actually follow him in?

00:42:15.880 --> 00:42:17.200
And the problem with that is

00:42:17.200 --> 00:42:19.260
the followup is really
important for these studies

00:42:19.260 --> 00:42:21.140
because if you don't have followup

00:42:21.140 --> 00:42:22.550
then you don't have internal validity.

00:42:22.550 --> 00:42:25.250
You just can't make any
conclusions about black men

00:42:25.250 --> 00:42:28.320
as a subcategory in these studies, okay?

00:42:28.320 --> 00:42:31.160
And so what we found first

00:42:31.160 --> 00:42:35.183
was these are the 25 biggest
studies that NHLBI funds.

00:42:36.800 --> 00:42:39.210
How many of them crystal do you think

00:42:39.210 --> 00:42:41.963
looked at incarcerated individuals?

00:42:44.706 --> 00:42:46.070
I'm putting you on the spot now.

00:42:46.070 --> 00:42:47.573
- No, no, you can't.

00:42:48.430 --> 00:42:53.090
What, let's say 10% of
them maybe you get them.

00:42:53.090 --> 00:42:57.310
- All right, zero, none, not a one, okay?

00:42:57.310 --> 00:43:00.310
One of them Cardia asks one question.

00:43:00.310 --> 00:43:01.760
It's been following people for 30 years.

00:43:01.760 --> 00:43:04.450
I mean, all the stuff that
we know about heart disease

00:43:04.450 --> 00:43:06.253
in young folks comes from Cardia.

00:43:07.650 --> 00:43:09.620
There was a big thing
about how much marijuana

00:43:09.620 --> 00:43:10.760
impacts your lung function

00:43:10.760 --> 00:43:11.810
that's coming from Cardia.

00:43:11.810 --> 00:43:14.890
Anything that has to do
with kind of cardiovascular

00:43:14.890 --> 00:43:18.090
and pulmonary health from
NHLBI comes from these studies.

00:43:18.090 --> 00:43:23.090
So zero go into prisons and
jails, one asks the question

00:43:23.730 --> 00:43:26.460
and then none of them follow people

00:43:26.460 --> 00:43:28.123
into prisons and jails either.

00:43:29.990 --> 00:43:32.120
So then we just try to
estimate how much the loss

00:43:32.120 --> 00:43:34.090
to follow up in these studies of black men

00:43:34.090 --> 00:43:35.263
might be attributable to incarceration

00:43:35.263 --> 00:43:37.300
because it couldn't actually measure it.

00:43:37.300 --> 00:43:38.820
And it turns out it's a good bit,

00:43:38.820 --> 00:43:41.460
so ranging from 15% and the lowest

00:43:41.460 --> 00:43:44.350
I'll have to put the
lowest at 5% to Cardia

00:43:44.350 --> 00:43:49.260
all the way up in black men to 25%, right?

00:43:49.260 --> 00:43:53.550
So that 25% of the
estimated loss to follow up,

00:43:53.550 --> 00:43:55.920
ugh, I'm actually not
amount reporting this well.

00:43:55.920 --> 00:43:57.790
So red is the actual loss to up,

00:43:57.790 --> 00:43:59.830
and this is how much of the loss to up

00:43:59.830 --> 00:44:01.740
can be attributed to incarceration.

00:44:01.740 --> 00:44:04.690
So in this study, it
looks like it's about 60%

00:44:04.690 --> 00:44:06.293
of the loss of the black men.

00:44:07.180 --> 00:44:09.470
And so it's just a good amount.

00:44:09.470 --> 00:44:12.083
It's a significant amount in some studies

00:44:12.083 --> 00:44:14.097
and what it speaks to me it's just that...

00:44:14.097 --> 00:44:16.730
Oh, that's a picture, a
sketch of one of my patients

00:44:16.730 --> 00:44:20.240
that he always loves me to
