00:52:34.130 --> 00:52:36.410
to other community, right?

00:52:36.410 --> 00:52:38.940
Three, it's partnership
throughout the process,

00:52:38.940 --> 00:52:40.610
it's negotiated throughout the process,

00:52:40.610 --> 00:52:42.070
it's discussed throughout the process,

00:52:42.070 --> 00:52:44.420
but it means in the first
articulation the question

00:52:44.420 --> 00:52:45.710
that comes from the community,

00:52:45.710 --> 00:52:47.219
then you find out the research question,

00:52:47.219 --> 00:52:49.510
then you figure out the
methods and protocols,

00:52:49.510 --> 00:52:50.960
then you're actually implementing it,

00:52:50.960 --> 00:52:52.370
then you're analyzing it

00:52:52.370 --> 00:52:55.360
and that's a place where
there's a lot of kind of

00:52:55.360 --> 00:52:57.750
like how many communities
actually have a statistician?

00:52:57.750 --> 00:52:58.850
You know what I mean?

00:52:58.850 --> 00:53:00.000
Like really to get to it,

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:01.820
so that's a place where
there's deep inequities

00:53:01.820 --> 00:53:03.340
and same with the cash

00:53:03.340 --> 00:53:06.020
and then the last is disseminating it.

00:53:06.020 --> 00:53:08.020
But in its fullest articulation,

00:53:08.020 --> 00:53:11.600
it is full participation
through the range.

00:53:11.600 --> 00:53:13.220
And I have to say it's not easy to do,

00:53:13.220 --> 00:53:15.000
so that's the first I'm gonna say.

00:53:15.000 --> 00:53:16.530
I will say,

00:53:16.530 --> 00:53:19.950
personally because the
structures don't permit it.

00:53:19.950 --> 00:53:24.570
So funding structures, and
also kind of deep inequities

00:53:24.570 --> 00:53:27.660
of like kind who can manage
and contractual obligations.

00:53:27.660 --> 00:53:30.870
We have tried to literally, like,

00:53:30.870 --> 00:53:32.787
yeah there's participatory
budgeting kind of

00:53:32.787 --> 00:53:36.530
and I think ours is a
really well-funded NIH lab

00:53:36.530 --> 00:53:41.530
and have worked and do
participatory budget.

00:53:41.700 --> 00:53:43.450
We're really transparent around it

00:53:43.450 --> 00:53:45.830
and what that means is talking
about really gross stuff,

00:53:45.830 --> 00:53:48.840
which is how much indirect
CL takes off of grants,

00:53:48.840 --> 00:53:50.563
how little communities will get,

00:53:51.608 --> 00:53:55.020
how to support and build
that infrastructure

00:53:55.020 --> 00:53:56.340
within community organizations

00:53:56.340 --> 00:53:59.210
that they can even take
an indirect, right?

00:53:59.210 --> 00:54:02.950
And that's where it has to go
in the deepest articulation.

00:54:02.950 --> 00:54:05.370
And also that's really complicated.

00:54:05.370 --> 00:54:08.460
We had one grant that we
were like, let's go 50, 50.

00:54:08.460 --> 00:54:10.120
We're gonna give you 2.5 million,

00:54:10.120 --> 00:54:11.900
we'll take the other 2.5 million

00:54:11.900 --> 00:54:14.880
that means that all faculty
salaries are gonna be squished,

00:54:14.880 --> 00:54:16.200
but we'll do it.

00:54:16.200 --> 00:54:17.420
And there was no community organization

00:54:17.420 --> 00:54:18.890
with the New Haven that could take it.

00:54:18.890 --> 00:54:22.570
It's too much work to take
on a real kind of like

00:54:24.730 --> 00:54:26.640
grassroots organization doesn't have

00:54:26.640 --> 00:54:28.840
the accounting infrastructure to do that.

00:54:28.840 --> 00:54:31.048
And so what that requires
are the infrastructures

00:54:31.048 --> 00:54:32.390
within the EL to do that.

00:54:32.390 --> 00:54:35.210
So we have a new office
of health equity research

00:54:35.210 --> 00:54:37.970
with a new Dean, a good friend of mine,

00:54:37.970 --> 00:54:40.990
Marcella Nunez-Smith,
I'm sure you'll know her.

00:54:40.990 --> 00:54:44.850
And that's finally, finally,

00:54:44.850 --> 00:54:46.680
we're gonna start trying to
build the infrastructure,

00:54:46.680 --> 00:54:50.400
but it takes kind of that to
be able to do it, that's one.

00:54:50.400 --> 00:54:51.970
And then the second I would say,

00:54:51.970 --> 00:54:56.030
is that it's deeply
enriching of the science.

00:54:56.030 --> 00:54:57.520
I can't tell you the number of times

00:54:57.520 --> 00:54:59.870
where you're like, my God, we're an idiot.

00:54:59.870 --> 00:55:02.190
We are asking all sorts
of the wrong questions

00:55:02.190 --> 00:55:04.460
or if we'd gone for that
standard validated measures,

00:55:04.460 --> 00:55:06.630
it would never have rolled out.

00:55:06.630 --> 00:55:08.590
And so it's the place where people think,

00:55:08.590 --> 00:55:11.763
oh, that's really nice, oh,
Emily's that left-leaning.

00:55:12.860 --> 00:55:16.490
It's what makes the science more accurate,

00:55:16.490 --> 00:55:18.750
more externally valid,
and internally valid.

00:55:18.750 --> 00:55:21.490
So I really push back on
notions that this is some

00:55:21.490 --> 00:55:25.620
kind of like left-leaning
progressive ways of including,

00:55:25.620 --> 00:55:26.760
that's crap.

00:55:26.760 --> 00:55:29.710
This is about the science and
it's about doing this right

00:55:29.710 --> 00:55:31.260
and it's about shifting power

00:55:31.260 --> 00:55:33.430
and also it's about making sure

00:55:33.430 --> 00:55:36.240
that what it is that you discover together

00:55:36.240 --> 00:55:38.230
actually is sustained beyond.

00:55:38.230 --> 00:55:40.380
And so that's in the fullest articulation.

00:55:40.380 --> 00:55:41.730
I'll just say this Crystal,

00:55:42.900 --> 00:55:44.830
not everything should
always be participatory,

00:55:44.830 --> 00:55:46.710
frankly, I think.

00:55:46.710 --> 00:55:49.640
There are places and spaces to do so.

00:55:49.640 --> 00:55:53.550
In my lab we'll be the first
to say, it's not easy work.

00:55:53.550 --> 00:55:55.410
So there's stuff that
we just have to get done

00:55:55.410 --> 00:55:57.430
and it's participatory, we're here kind of

00:55:57.430 --> 00:55:59.430
but there's, we all have
our different expertise

00:55:59.430 --> 00:56:00.410
and we're okay with that.

00:56:00.410 --> 00:56:02.010
So that's the second thing I'll say

00:56:02.010 --> 00:56:04.330
and then the work that
we're doing in New Haven.

00:56:04.330 --> 00:56:07.360
So clinically within the SEICHE Center,

00:56:07.360 --> 00:56:09.020
our grounding is our clinical work.

00:56:09.020 --> 00:56:10.370
Like that informs everything

00:56:10.370 --> 00:56:12.430
'cause otherwise you can't
keep yourself very real

00:56:12.430 --> 00:56:13.693
even with lab members.

00:56:16.800 --> 00:56:18.430
And so New Haven we have

00:56:18.430 --> 00:56:23.170
a kind of a Transitions
Clinic Network of...

00:56:24.438 --> 00:56:26.440
There's 45 programs across the country,

00:56:26.440 --> 00:56:29.230
but there's three sites in Connecticut

00:56:29.230 --> 00:56:32.710
and the research that we have

00:56:32.710 --> 00:56:35.790
is mostly embedded within Connecticut.

00:56:35.790 --> 00:56:37.860
It's where we have the
strongest relationships

00:56:37.860 --> 00:56:39.590
where the assets are the greatest.

00:56:39.590 --> 00:56:43.780
And so, I'll just point out
the participatory project

00:56:43.780 --> 00:56:45.570
that we have on-going
and I'm looking at staff

00:56:45.570 --> 00:56:49.782
who's been involved since really early on

00:56:49.782 --> 00:56:51.730
and at least then the NIH grant.

00:56:51.730 --> 00:56:53.570
So we had a nine year partnership,

00:56:53.570 --> 00:56:55.840
a community academic partnership

00:56:55.840 --> 00:56:57.710
that's been focused on gun violence.

00:56:57.710 --> 00:57:00.520
And in particular thinking about
community-led interventions

00:57:00.520 --> 00:57:02.810
that can mitigate and ultimately prevent

00:57:02.810 --> 00:57:05.583
the community impacts of gun violence.

00:57:06.560 --> 00:57:09.660
We were awarded an NIH
funding in that brief period

00:57:09.660 --> 00:57:13.250
where they had kind of an
RFA focused on gun violence.

00:57:13.250 --> 00:57:14.980
And what we've been able to do

00:57:14.980 --> 00:57:17.060
is apply kind of a
social network analysis.

00:57:17.060 --> 00:57:19.640
So there was a sociologist here

00:57:19.640 --> 00:57:22.130
that I used to be here,
Andrew Papa Christos

00:57:22.130 --> 00:57:23.880
that had a social network analysis

00:57:23.880 --> 00:57:26.920
and did that using arrest data

00:57:26.920 --> 00:57:29.150
and we tried to incorporate
health system data

00:57:29.150 --> 00:57:33.290
into the social networks of gun violence.

00:57:33.290 --> 00:57:37.250
And we took his data and what
we were really interested

00:57:37.250 --> 00:57:40.279
like I said earlier is a
positive deviants model.

00:57:40.279 --> 00:57:43.540
So, all sorts of folks are
focused on this high risk people.

00:57:43.540 --> 00:57:45.810
Who I'm interested in are the people

00:57:45.810 --> 00:57:48.310
that actually are looking
like they're high risk

00:57:48.310 --> 00:57:49.970
and never get shot.

00:57:49.970 --> 00:57:52.080
What are they doing right,
what's helping them?

00:57:52.080 --> 00:57:53.470
the spaces in the community.

00:57:53.470 --> 00:57:55.270
There's lots of places that look like

00:57:55.270 --> 00:57:58.350
they should have had a ton
of shootings and they didn't,

00:57:58.350 --> 00:58:00.010
statistically, right?

00:58:00.010 --> 00:58:01.450
What's going on right?

00:58:01.450 --> 00:58:03.810
And so we use that social network analysis

00:58:03.810 --> 00:58:06.000
and this is all in partnership.

00:58:06.000 --> 00:58:07.770
I'm telling you, we've
gone nine years with it

00:58:07.770 --> 00:58:09.550
and this is the group that we're
trying to give out the cash

00:58:09.550 --> 00:58:10.383
and they couldn't do it.

00:58:10.383 --> 00:58:12.340
So they're like, oh my God, another grant.

00:58:12.340 --> 00:58:14.010
I'm like, come on, where could they?

00:58:14.010 --> 00:58:16.740
So then did qualitative interviews

00:58:16.740 --> 00:58:19.000
of either individuals or those places

00:58:19.000 --> 00:58:20.559
where things are working

00:58:20.559 --> 00:58:23.190
or the places where nothing's happening

00:58:23.190 --> 00:58:25.603
and Steph led a lot of that work.

00:58:26.490 --> 00:58:30.400
And then now are using
both quantitative data

00:58:30.400 --> 00:58:32.910
from New Haven, the interviews

00:58:32.910 --> 00:58:36.020
and using this thing called
systems dynamic modeling.

00:58:36.020 --> 00:58:39.120
So it's kind of newer in medicine,

00:58:39.120 --> 00:58:40.250
not a lot of folks are using,

00:58:40.250 --> 00:58:43.700
but it's a way of building a model,

00:58:43.700 --> 00:58:45.750
a computer simulated model

00:58:45.750 --> 00:58:48.670
where you create inputs that give

00:58:48.670 --> 00:58:52.070
where you put in local data, okay.

00:58:52.070 --> 00:58:56.420
And then can simulate and
dialing up and dialing down

00:58:58.330 --> 00:59:01.020
different factors that
might impact gun violence.

00:59:01.020 --> 00:59:02.170
So for instance,

00:59:02.170 --> 00:59:05.770
okay, and I'll just give you
an example from our work.

00:59:05.770 --> 00:59:07.850
The process starts with something called

00:59:07.850 --> 00:59:09.090
group participatory modeling

00:59:09.090 --> 00:59:11.330
where basically this community
academic partnerships,

00:59:11.330 --> 00:59:13.870
so they've now gotten
all used to the terms

00:59:13.870 --> 00:59:15.720
have to really think hard about

00:59:15.720 --> 00:59:18.330
what are the different inputs
that cause gun violence

00:59:18.330 --> 00:59:20.730
in our community and in
particular not about gun violence,

00:59:20.730 --> 00:59:22.310
but the trauma that lingers?

00:59:22.310 --> 00:59:24.230
Okay, so they create this model

00:59:24.230 --> 00:59:25.590
and one of the big things on there

00:59:25.590 --> 00:59:28.330
let's say is green spaces, okay.

00:59:28.330 --> 00:59:29.750
And that's been born
out in the literature,

00:59:29.750 --> 00:59:31.500
so of course these folks
have thought about it,

00:59:31.500 --> 00:59:34.210
but there's a good rich
empiric science behind it.

00:59:34.210 --> 00:59:35.930
And then you put it in your model

00:59:35.930 --> 00:59:38.890
and what you can do using New
Haven data is just saying,

00:59:38.890 --> 00:59:42.380
what if we tweaked up green
spaces in New Haven by 20%,

00:59:42.380 --> 00:59:44.280
what happens to rates of gun violence?

00:59:45.120 --> 00:59:48.640
And so now the community members
can see this model, use it,

00:59:48.640 --> 00:59:50.730
think about the factors that
they think are important

00:59:50.730 --> 00:59:52.340
to tweak up and tweak down.

00:59:52.340 --> 00:59:54.740
And part of what we're trying to identify

00:59:54.740 --> 00:59:57.830
are interventions that could
be community led entirely,

00:59:57.830 --> 01:00:01.150
not dependent on government,
not dependent on police.

01:00:01.150 --> 01:00:04.960
That they could lead on
their own to take action

01:00:04.960 --> 01:00:07.890
to take hold of something that

01:00:07.890 --> 01:00:09.530
they urgently need interventions

01:00:09.530 --> 01:00:12.430
like every government cycle
and every budget shortfall

01:00:12.430 --> 01:00:14.423
or whatever they're done with that.

01:00:18.110 --> 01:00:20.540
It's complicated and I just grant cycles

01:00:20.540 --> 01:00:21.850
of three years of five years

01:00:21.850 --> 01:00:23.840
and we're now at that place

01:00:23.840 --> 01:00:26.480
where we're coming up
with the intervention

01:00:26.480 --> 01:00:29.383
and it's both exciting, but it's also,

01:00:30.880 --> 01:00:34.210
sometimes science is
slow, it gets old, right?

01:00:34.210 --> 01:00:37.570
The rates of gun violence are
going up and I tire of it,

01:00:37.570 --> 01:00:39.160
but I also just remind myself

01:00:39.160 --> 01:00:42.160
that if we don't do this well and right,

01:00:42.160 --> 01:00:44.280
that I think it's gonna be the same crap.

01:00:44.280 --> 01:00:47.490
We just don't have
solutions that are actually

01:00:47.490 --> 01:00:50.900
with rigorous evidence behind
it that'll end up being scaled

01:00:50.900 --> 01:00:53.650
and then we end up with more of the same,

01:00:53.650 --> 01:00:57.420
which is like, if we tiled
down police, dial up police,

01:00:57.420 --> 01:00:58.970
community led police.

01:00:58.970 --> 01:01:00.540
And it's not to say that those solutions

01:01:00.540 --> 01:01:02.110
I mean, that's not the
conversation I entertained

01:01:02.110 --> 01:01:03.040
that's for somebody else.

01:01:03.040 --> 01:01:06.870
Our lab is squarely focused on
what works within communities

01:01:06.870 --> 01:01:08.790
and how can community leaders

01:01:08.790 --> 01:01:13.550
kind of envision their
own safety and own health.

01:01:13.550 --> 01:01:18.050
- This is amazing, Emily
and I'm learning a lot.

01:01:18.050 --> 01:01:20.957
And I wanna bring in a few
of the other questions-

01:01:20.957 --> 01:01:22.040
- Yeah, sure.

01:01:23.210 --> 01:01:25.620
- They range, so I'm
trying to consolidate some.

01:01:25.620 --> 01:01:27.810
But there was a question about

01:01:27.810 --> 01:01:31.410
when you were talking about
health as a constitutional right

01:01:31.410 --> 01:01:35.040
and someone asked explicitly
about mental health

01:01:35.040 --> 01:01:38.700
and how that factors in to
folks who are incarcerated.

01:01:38.700 --> 01:01:42.610
Is mental health apart of
that constitutional right

01:01:42.610 --> 01:01:47.610
and what does it look like in the prisons?

01:01:48.010 --> 01:01:50.240
- Yeah, so I don't know the court case,

01:01:50.240 --> 01:01:53.580
but yes, it is part of
that constitutional right.

01:01:53.580 --> 01:01:55.040
And then I will say,

01:01:55.040 --> 01:01:57.210
healthcare throughout prisons and jails,

01:01:57.210 --> 01:02:01.160
there's a one, a real
heterogeneity to that care

01:02:01.160 --> 01:02:04.190
and two, we just don't know.

01:02:04.190 --> 01:02:07.723
It's like what we know is that anecdotal,

01:02:08.630 --> 01:02:11.247
but what I've seen also over time,

01:02:11.247 --> 01:02:15.357
and I've seen really I
think like easily thousands

01:02:15.357 --> 01:02:20.357
is that prisons and jails
are in and of themselves

01:02:21.940 --> 01:02:23.230
trauma inducing.

01:02:23.230 --> 01:02:27.540
And for people that have had either

01:02:27.540 --> 01:02:31.150
go into prisons and jails
for mental health disorders

01:02:31.150 --> 01:02:33.830
or have had significant traumas

01:02:33.830 --> 01:02:37.300
and the life course of people
that have been incarcerated

01:02:37.300 --> 01:02:42.300
is predominantly, incredibly
challenged, incredibly violent

01:02:43.670 --> 01:02:46.230
and then to enter spaces
that are also violent

01:02:46.230 --> 01:02:47.963
in their kind of existence.

01:02:49.283 --> 01:02:52.398
Yeah, I think if there
are solid literature

01:02:52.398 --> 01:02:53.790
around kind of the health impacts

01:02:53.790 --> 01:02:55.640
of actually being in the carceral system

01:02:55.640 --> 01:02:58.400
it's around mental health
outcomes and it's terrible.

01:02:58.400 --> 01:03:01.240
And so to get into the nitty gritty,

01:03:01.240 --> 01:03:04.583
what I would say though, is
it is really complicated.

01:03:06.110 --> 01:03:09.000
To make, I don't know if I
should go into this tangent,

01:03:09.000 --> 01:03:13.490
but to make a mental health diagnoses

01:03:13.490 --> 01:03:15.850
and I'll just use the example of psychosis

01:03:15.850 --> 01:03:18.130
like having kind of a...

01:03:18.130 --> 01:03:19.450
And you know, I'm an internist,

01:03:19.450 --> 01:03:22.950
so I'll just say that this
is not like a psychiatrist

01:03:22.950 --> 01:03:27.540
would be like, she's not
really doing it justice.

01:03:27.540 --> 01:03:30.380
But to just say this is that,

01:03:30.380 --> 01:03:33.110
oftentimes making the
diagnoses within prisons

01:03:33.110 --> 01:03:35.050
and different diagnoses in community,

01:03:35.050 --> 01:03:36.670
it's just very difficult.

01:03:36.670 --> 01:03:38.390
So, there's a clear-cut way

01:03:38.390 --> 01:03:41.460
of diagnosing high blood pressure, right?

01:03:41.460 --> 01:03:43.740
But there isn't a, you know,
even though there's guidelines

01:03:43.740 --> 01:03:46.690
of like a DSM-5 criteria
and we go through it

01:03:46.690 --> 01:03:49.590
kind of the conditions
of the system inside

01:03:49.590 --> 01:03:51.994
and oftentimes people
have used an illicit drug

01:03:51.994 --> 01:03:56.030
while they're outside really,
really affect those diagnoses

01:03:56.030 --> 01:03:58.730
and so there's just so much disconnect

01:03:58.730 --> 01:03:59.960
between these health systems.

01:03:59.960 --> 01:04:02.340
It's a person will be on
a mental health, you know

01:04:02.340 --> 01:04:07.050
drug, treatment, engaged in
care, outside in the inside

01:04:07.050 --> 01:04:10.240
not be seen in the same
way, you hear them saying,

01:04:10.240 --> 01:04:14.880
and vice versa, drug, treated,
engaged in care, come home

01:04:14.880 --> 01:04:16.110
and they're like, we don't see.

01:04:16.110 --> 01:04:20.050
And so I think it's just
like, I can't imagine,

01:04:20.050 --> 01:04:21.730
like I would just be so frustrated

01:04:21.730 --> 01:04:23.570
'cause you're like I was doing well here

01:04:23.570 --> 01:04:25.670
and then now I've come
in and there's nothing

01:04:25.670 --> 01:04:28.130
and or I was doing well inside actually

01:04:28.130 --> 01:04:29.020
and that does happen.

01:04:29.020 --> 01:04:31.000
Like I've just shared with
you guys, it's against,

01:04:31.000 --> 01:04:33.930
it's counter to what we think,
but some people's health,

01:04:33.930 --> 01:04:36.380
it's not to say that we
should incarcerate people,

01:04:36.380 --> 01:04:39.030
but some people's help
actually are managed,

01:04:39.030 --> 01:04:40.910
surprisingly when they actually have food

01:04:40.910 --> 01:04:42.250
and a roof over their heads.

01:04:42.250 --> 01:04:45.310
So it managed better and then come home

01:04:45.310 --> 01:04:48.500
and they're just so aggravated
by not having Medicaid,

01:04:48.500 --> 01:04:50.202
not having Medicare,
losing their benefits,

01:04:50.202 --> 01:04:52.400
they're homeless, et cetera.

01:04:52.400 --> 01:04:56.380
So that is a really
complicated place in part,

01:04:59.370 --> 01:05:02.140
because of real breaks and care

01:05:02.140 --> 01:05:04.700
in part by kind of who the providers are,

01:05:04.700 --> 01:05:06.080
how they see the person,

01:05:06.080 --> 01:05:09.000
and how they see kind
of severity of illness

01:05:09.000 --> 01:05:10.963
in the certain circumstances.

01:05:12.720 --> 01:05:13.693
- Thank you.

01:05:14.740 --> 01:05:17.290
Now I have a question, it's quite there

01:05:17.290 --> 01:05:21.290
it's specific around someone
who's been doing stuff

01:05:21.290 --> 01:05:24.690
with the Connecticut Bail Fund hotline.

01:05:24.690 --> 01:05:28.360
And one of the things that
they're hearing a lot about is

01:05:28.360 --> 01:05:33.000
where in prison physicians
are sometimes complicit

01:05:33.000 --> 01:05:37.407
and punishing people and withholding care

01:05:39.550 --> 01:05:40.620
and things like that.

01:05:40.620 --> 01:05:44.830
And the person is wandering,
is there something that the lab

01:05:44.830 --> 01:05:49.830
or the center is gonna do to
address some of those issues

01:05:51.670 --> 01:05:53.913
that are happening within prisons?

01:05:55.910 --> 01:05:58.220
- I guess, what I would say is that

01:05:58.220 --> 01:06:01.849
what we do clinically is, if
we see a patient in our clinic

01:06:01.849 --> 01:06:04.270
and then we see bad care

01:06:04.270 --> 01:06:07.000
that, of course, it's incumbent upon us

01:06:07.000 --> 01:06:09.430
to then talk to the provider, figure out,

01:06:09.430 --> 01:06:12.490
and see what it is that we do.

01:06:12.490 --> 01:06:14.280
Within a research, that's not a place

01:06:14.280 --> 01:06:15.720
that we're actively investigating

01:06:15.720 --> 01:06:19.840
and places that but I do think
it's of critical importance.

01:06:19.840 --> 01:06:24.400
I think oversight is, as
I indicated there's not.

01:06:24.400 --> 01:06:28.563
Right, there's no mandatory
oversight, it blows my mind.

01:06:29.540 --> 01:06:32.830
We just kind of absolved
ourselves of responsibility.

01:06:32.830 --> 01:06:34.190
We don't wanna see it, we
don't wanna care about it.

01:06:34.190 --> 01:06:35.933
It just, I can't believe that,

01:06:37.860 --> 01:06:40.450
as a community, this is
something that we stand for it,

01:06:40.450 --> 01:06:44.170
but it is not a place of
active advocacy right now,

01:06:44.170 --> 01:06:46.200
for our center.

01:06:46.200 --> 01:06:48.530
The things that we're really focused on

01:06:48.530 --> 01:06:50.588
partially because of

01:06:50.588 --> 01:06:52.900
where we think we're gonna
have the most leverage

01:06:52.900 --> 01:06:54.330
and maybe this is just strategic

01:06:54.330 --> 01:06:58.460
and also just the real deep
importance of decarceration

01:06:58.460 --> 01:07:00.420
is trying to change compassionate release

01:07:00.420 --> 01:07:04.170
like trying to really shore
up the science around it,

01:07:04.170 --> 01:07:06.180
try to change the policies.

01:07:06.180 --> 01:07:08.320
It's almost impossible to get a person

01:07:08.320 --> 01:07:10.490
to be released in this country.

01:07:10.490 --> 01:07:14.130
And the consensus report
we just did from NISM

01:07:14.130 --> 01:07:19.130
said that one of the things
that is clear is that about 20,

01:07:19.240 --> 01:07:21.970
the jail populations are down about 22%,

01:07:21.970 --> 01:07:23.660
pre COVID and post COVID,

01:07:23.660 --> 01:07:26.840
but the state prison
population is down only 4%.

01:07:26.840 --> 01:07:29.700
And even in settings where the executive,

01:07:29.700 --> 01:07:34.080
the governor, et cetera, wanted
to release, it's hard to do.

01:07:34.080 --> 01:07:36.950
The compassionate, at least
policies are too specific,

01:07:36.950 --> 01:07:40.190
too discretionary, really difficult to do

01:07:40.190 --> 01:07:42.330
to actually see it through

01:07:42.330 --> 01:07:45.283
and so these are the things
that we're working on.

01:07:46.260 --> 01:07:49.010
- And I had another question
here, I'm gonna read it, okay.

01:07:49.010 --> 01:07:52.010
It said, it's been
striking to me how so much

01:07:52.010 --> 01:07:55.590
of the conservative political
discussions around healthcare

01:07:55.590 --> 01:07:57.870
this year with the
conservative politicians

01:07:57.870 --> 01:08:01.210
and protestors reactions
to mask in the pandemic

01:08:01.210 --> 01:08:03.880
and in general regarding
conservative rejections

01:08:03.880 --> 01:08:05.240
of Obamacare.

01:08:05.240 --> 01:08:09.290
Employees the language of force
and encouraging on Liberty.

01:08:09.290 --> 01:08:10.963
It's been especially striking this year

01:08:10.963 --> 01:08:13.690
against the juxtaposition of incarcerated

01:08:13.690 --> 01:08:15.450
and detained individuals

01:08:15.450 --> 01:08:17.670
whose health has actually been threatened

01:08:17.670 --> 01:08:20.930
by the limitations of
their liberty and mobility.

01:08:20.930 --> 01:08:24.720
What reconceptualization
of health, public health,

01:08:24.720 --> 01:08:27.830
the common good, and freedom,
do you see as necessary

01:08:27.830 --> 01:08:30.020
to change in public conversations

01:08:30.020 --> 01:08:33.260
and politics around
health in this country?

01:08:33.260 --> 01:08:34.930
- Well, I have to say, I don't know who,

01:08:34.930 --> 01:08:36.400
and I'm gonna say her.

01:08:36.400 --> 01:08:39.090
This person (indistinct) Gina Moore,

01:08:39.090 --> 01:08:40.600
but I'm just gonna say this.

01:08:40.600 --> 01:08:42.730
Man, if you had an idea,
you got to share it

01:08:42.730 --> 01:08:44.390
because I think that that's literally

01:08:44.390 --> 01:08:46.590
what we spend our time thinking most about

01:08:46.590 --> 01:08:50.583
is how can we make this an
issue that is everyone's issue.

01:08:55.770 --> 01:08:58.220
I guess like the ways
that we've thought about

01:08:58.220 --> 01:09:03.143
it is that core to us is as I shared,

01:09:07.940 --> 01:09:09.800
and I think I'm reminded daily.

01:09:09.800 --> 01:09:11.890
There's a deep urgency to this work

01:09:11.890 --> 01:09:15.660
and it's urgent from some
people more than others,

01:09:15.660 --> 01:09:17.240
but I feel like because I see patients

01:09:17.240 --> 01:09:20.480
and then my lab has always that it's,

01:09:20.480 --> 01:09:22.330
this is a matter of survival, right?

01:09:22.330 --> 01:09:26.660
And so trying to think about
the right sorts of questions

01:09:26.660 --> 01:09:31.300
to expand this to include
others, I think it's important.

01:09:31.300 --> 01:09:35.303
It also, I think is, should I say this?

01:09:41.550 --> 01:09:43.430
It's not a surprise, I also,

01:09:45.711 --> 01:09:48.600
I think it's slightly loathsome too

01:09:49.510 --> 01:09:51.060
that there's increased interest

01:09:51.060 --> 01:09:53.950
in thinking about
criminal justice policies

01:09:53.950 --> 01:09:56.255
given the opioid epidemic.

01:09:56.255 --> 01:10:00.670
And focus now that white communities,

01:10:00.670 --> 01:10:01.900
rural communities are interested.

01:10:01.900 --> 01:10:04.798
These are places of urgent intervention.

01:10:04.798 --> 01:10:06.750
It's also a place where we feel like,

01:10:06.750 --> 01:10:08.850
well, then we just have
to be strategic about it.

01:10:08.850 --> 01:10:11.760
And so I think our lab
really thinks hard about

01:10:11.760 --> 01:10:13.720
how it is that we can
broaden the conversation

01:10:13.720 --> 01:10:17.080
include more people we've been at

01:10:17.080 --> 01:10:18.790
increasingly some of the research

01:10:18.790 --> 01:10:20.710
that you'll see coming out of our lab

01:10:20.710 --> 01:10:21.923
is focused on families.

01:10:23.580 --> 01:10:26.400
There's beautiful work done
by Chris Waldman's team

01:10:26.400 --> 01:10:29.300
in Cornell, where he finally
created a national estimate

01:10:29.300 --> 01:10:32.650
of how many people have a family member

01:10:32.650 --> 01:10:34.340
that's been exposed to incarceration.

01:10:34.340 --> 01:10:37.320
And so in this country I don't
know if y'all have seen it,

01:10:37.320 --> 01:10:39.820
but it's almost half, right?

01:10:39.820 --> 01:10:40.920
And then among black family,

01:10:40.920 --> 01:10:42.740
black communities it's even higher.

01:10:42.740 --> 01:10:47.380
And so we've done a study
using the data our FAM has

01:10:47.380 --> 01:10:49.203
I think he's publicly shared it.

01:10:50.130 --> 01:10:52.550
And we've embedded in the
measures of wellbeing.

01:10:52.550 --> 01:10:56.150
And what we found was
that pretty profound,

01:10:56.150 --> 01:10:59.300
but even if you had an
extended family member,

01:10:59.300 --> 01:11:02.700
that's incarcerated a cousin,
a godparent, whatever,

01:11:02.700 --> 01:11:05.320
your wellbeing is worse than those

01:11:05.320 --> 01:11:07.970
that have never had a person
with a history incarceration

01:11:07.970 --> 01:11:09.420
and this wellbeing measure that we use

01:11:09.420 --> 01:11:11.693
translates to life expectancy.

01:11:12.580 --> 01:11:14.730
I think lot of it is just thinking about

01:11:14.730 --> 01:11:18.150
the other stakeholders that
are impacted, family members.

01:11:18.150 --> 01:11:20.620
One of our team members is now again,

01:11:20.620 --> 01:11:22.390
within the context of COVID,

01:11:22.390 --> 01:11:25.130
starting to think about kind
of correctional officers.

01:11:25.130 --> 01:11:28.850
So I recently learned that their mean,

01:11:28.850 --> 01:11:30.520
I don't know if this should be quoted

01:11:30.520 --> 01:11:32.000
because I don't know
this to be a true fact,

01:11:32.000 --> 01:11:36.440
but I heard that mean
life expectancy is 66,

01:11:36.440 --> 01:11:38.500
so it's crazy low.

01:11:38.500 --> 01:11:40.250
And I think it's because they work

01:11:40.250 --> 01:11:42.480
in toxic stressful environments.

01:11:42.480 --> 01:11:46.070
The same toxicity that our
patients feel, they feel too

01:11:46.070 --> 01:11:49.370
and it's a way of also
bringing in other people

01:11:49.370 --> 01:11:52.120
into the conversation,
COVID is doing that for us.

01:11:52.120 --> 01:11:55.260
So it's trying to bring into
the conversation other people

01:11:56.520 --> 01:11:58.850
who haven't always been
in the conversation

01:11:58.850 --> 01:12:00.570
of how it is that we decarcerate.

01:12:00.570 --> 01:12:03.130
And so we're really trying to work on that

01:12:03.130 --> 01:12:08.130
and where we're best is
both doing the research

01:12:08.390 --> 01:12:13.160
and then trying to think
about more legal avenues now

01:12:13.160 --> 01:12:15.443
of trying to expand that conversation.

01:12:19.870 --> 01:12:21.950
