01:15:48.520 --> 01:15:52.390
And so us in the community
of African-American Studies

01:15:52.390 --> 01:15:55.350
what can we do to support this work?

01:15:55.350 --> 01:15:58.610
- Well, it's interesting,
I have to say this.

01:16:00.740 --> 01:16:03.350
I only recently started reading history

01:16:03.350 --> 01:16:04.550
of the Freedman's Bureau

01:16:06.920 --> 01:16:09.790
and it's in that reading that I realized

01:16:12.380 --> 01:16:16.610
it's a deeply challenged history.

01:16:16.610 --> 01:16:18.460
It was the first,

01:16:18.460 --> 01:16:23.080
if I'm understanding national
health system in our country

01:16:23.930 --> 01:16:28.460
and was grossly underfunded
and has so many deep parallels

01:16:28.460 --> 01:16:31.250
because it was essentially a
second class healthcare system

01:16:31.250 --> 01:16:35.228
for certain sorts of people,
you know, former slaves.

01:16:35.228 --> 01:16:36.700
And it's similar,

01:16:36.700 --> 01:16:41.700
like we've constructed a whole
other operating health system

01:16:42.224 --> 01:16:44.490
that is totally out of you,

01:16:44.490 --> 01:16:46.871
that's financed by our collective dollars

01:16:46.871 --> 01:16:49.030
that we've just thrown it away.

01:16:49.030 --> 01:16:51.070
And it has differential impacts

01:16:51.070 --> 01:16:53.120
across kind of our communities,

01:16:53.120 --> 01:16:54.770
but it's a place that I think

01:16:54.770 --> 01:16:57.673
really requires deep inquiry now.

01:16:57.673 --> 01:17:01.033
What I'm interested in seeing
are kind of how health help,

01:17:02.430 --> 01:17:06.500
how doctors help allied professionals

01:17:06.500 --> 01:17:10.850
like how are we complicit in
that, bringing that history,

01:17:10.850 --> 01:17:12.363
Where are places?

01:17:13.940 --> 01:17:18.740
I just haven't seen, and
again I just added myself,

01:17:18.740 --> 01:17:20.240
which is like I've just newly

01:17:20.240 --> 01:17:21.620
started reading about
the Freedmen's Bureau.

01:17:21.620 --> 01:17:23.140
So you hear what I'm saying,

01:17:23.140 --> 01:17:26.150
it's maybe that there's a ton out there,

01:17:26.150 --> 01:17:29.970
but when we turn to our
colleagues within history

01:17:29.970 --> 01:17:33.080
and legal scholars and
then even Miriam Rich,

01:17:33.080 --> 01:17:36.290
who's at the history of
medicine with in Yale

01:17:36.290 --> 01:17:39.560
there isn't a lot as far as she can tell.

01:17:39.560 --> 01:17:41.610
And she's a scholar of mass incarceration

01:17:41.610 --> 01:17:43.040
and the intersections of help

01:17:43.040 --> 01:17:45.280
of how physicians are
complicit or haven't been,

01:17:45.280 --> 01:17:46.730
how have they been revolutionary?

01:17:46.730 --> 01:17:48.350
We need narratives that help

01:17:48.350 --> 01:17:50.800
kind of make this our own issue.

01:17:50.800 --> 01:17:54.560
And so I kind of would turn
it to you, which is that

01:17:54.560 --> 01:17:56.920
I think with the work that
you do is illuminating.

01:17:56.920 --> 01:18:01.890
It gives us a grounding
for what is possible,

01:18:01.890 --> 01:18:04.500
what had been possible,
what's deeply flawed.

01:18:04.500 --> 01:18:05.810
It also makes us realize

01:18:05.810 --> 01:18:08.750
like in turning to a
historical perspective,

01:18:08.750 --> 01:18:13.310
this stuff isn't new,
I mean, just isn't now.

01:18:13.310 --> 01:18:16.070
You know it's like kind of popped up again

01:18:16.070 --> 01:18:20.500
and for me, I think
especially for generations

01:18:20.500 --> 01:18:22.930
of new med students
they think, oh my gosh,

01:18:22.930 --> 01:18:25.560
this is the first time and
its own, and we just have to

01:18:25.560 --> 01:18:28.170
I think that there also
gonna be stories out there

01:18:28.170 --> 01:18:31.660
within history of solutions
of radical uprisings

01:18:31.660 --> 01:18:35.450
and ways that physicians,
ways that health systems

01:18:35.450 --> 01:18:38.470
or more grassroots health systems

01:18:38.470 --> 01:18:40.090
have come up to fight this.

01:18:40.090 --> 01:18:42.570
And to me, those stories have to be told

01:18:42.570 --> 01:18:45.903
for us to kind of embrace
a fuller and richer

01:18:49.220 --> 01:18:54.220
and create a more honest
narrative of who we are,

01:18:56.310 --> 01:18:57.920
how we are as help communities,

01:18:57.920 --> 01:19:01.340
and how we've really have never

01:19:01.340 --> 01:19:04.133
cared for black people in this country?

01:19:05.801 --> 01:19:09.070
I just invite you guys to step
foot into prisons and jails.

01:19:09.070 --> 01:19:12.143
That to me is like, I feel
like the first time I did it,

01:19:12.143 --> 01:19:14.253
I just couldn't turn back.

01:19:15.960 --> 01:19:17.660
And then I've been to prisons and jails

01:19:17.660 --> 01:19:19.820
all over now, the world

01:19:19.820 --> 01:19:24.317
and so I have to say it doesn't matter

01:19:24.317 --> 01:19:26.430
and it's just so deeply.

01:19:26.430 --> 01:19:28.823
We've created a system that really,

01:19:31.080 --> 01:19:31.913
and I don't know,

01:19:31.913 --> 01:19:34.690
the last patient I just felt
come home from prison was 75,

01:19:35.900 --> 01:19:38.267
spent 40 years inside.

01:19:38.267 --> 01:19:42.060
I mean, that just can't be this, you know?

01:19:42.060 --> 01:19:44.682
40 years inside during COVID.

01:19:44.682 --> 01:19:48.010
So I guess that that's what I'd say

01:19:48.010 --> 01:19:50.913
is there's a lot to be done.

01:19:52.570 --> 01:19:54.160
Doctors are real pragmatic, I guess,

01:19:54.160 --> 01:19:56.720
so I'm like, well, there's
stuff that you can do now later,

01:19:56.720 --> 01:19:58.590
but I think like for me, I mean,

01:19:58.590 --> 01:20:00.382
and then we welcome kind of,

01:20:00.382 --> 01:20:03.910
come over to our side of the campus.

01:20:03.910 --> 01:20:07.662
There's a lot going on here and we have,

01:20:07.662 --> 01:20:12.620
I look at Crystal, but
you know we've developed

01:20:12.620 --> 01:20:14.313
and have growing relationships,

01:20:14.313 --> 01:20:19.230
certainly with folks on the FIS side.

01:20:19.230 --> 01:20:21.420
But our strongest reach
right now at the law school,

01:20:21.420 --> 01:20:26.420
but would love real
partnerships across Yale

01:20:26.740 --> 01:20:30.024
because I do think like,
especially just thinking about

01:20:30.024 --> 01:20:33.760
who's coming next step
in your Endeavors series.

01:20:33.760 --> 01:20:35.560
I mean, this is a place

01:20:35.560 --> 01:20:39.940
where I think Yale has
many, many assets to offer.

01:20:39.940 --> 01:20:42.910
How we reimagine and reshape conversations

01:20:42.910 --> 01:20:47.060
around what a decarcerate
system looks like.

01:20:47.060 --> 01:20:49.910
- Thank you, I think
that is a powerful note

01:20:49.910 --> 01:20:52.920
to end on an amazing invitation,

01:20:52.920 --> 01:20:54.660
particularly to our graduate students

01:20:54.660 --> 01:20:57.850
who are doing creative
and imaginative work

01:20:57.850 --> 01:21:01.830
on policing, on the environment,

01:21:01.830 --> 01:21:06.830
and how the carceral state
has impacted black lives.

01:21:07.200 --> 01:21:11.160
And that I know that some
of them after the day

01:21:11.160 --> 01:21:15.770
will be drawn to the center
and I will encourage them

01:21:15.770 --> 01:21:20.770
and my colleagues to
engage in a conversation

01:21:21.160 --> 01:21:22.470
with you and your work.

01:21:22.470 --> 01:21:25.070
So again, thank you for
sharing this with us,

01:21:25.070 --> 01:21:25.980
we've learned so much.

01:21:25.980 --> 01:21:27.900
I'm gonna ask people to unmute

01:21:27.900 --> 01:21:30.090
in this moment before you go off

01:21:30.090 --> 01:21:34.763
and we're gonna do a clap that
you can actually hear, right?

01:21:34.763 --> 01:21:35.839
Instead of,
(claps)

01:21:35.839 --> 01:21:38.156
So iF people will unmute and clap

01:21:38.156 --> 01:21:39.927
that would be great.

01:21:39.927 --> 01:21:44.559
Thank you so much.
(chimes)

